@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl Conjecture2
-- L4DEL: title: Reconstructing a typed bipartite graph from its incidence-deletion deck
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let `G` and `H` use the same finite bipartition. If `G` remains connected after deleting any vertex, its vertex types are pairwise distinct, and the two incidence-deletion decks agree, then a bijection preserving the two parts also preserves every edge. Thus the deck determines `G` up to bipartite isomorphism under these strong distinguishability hypotheses.
-- L4DEL: step: Equality of decks pairs every incidence-deleted card of `G` with a bipartite-isomorphic card of `H`.
-- L4DEL: step: From those paired cards the proof recovers degrees, degree profiles, vertex types, and then each vertex's multiset of neighbouring types.
-- L4DEL: step: Pairwise distinct vertex types make adjacency readable from the neighbouring-type multiset, so the card pairing extends to a bipartition-preserving graph isomorphism.
-- L4DEL: step: The Lean statement also accepts `G_not_regular`, but the proof term never uses it and the companion theorem does not assume non-regularity. It is therefore a redundant formal hypothesis, not part of the mathematical mechanism described here.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Paul J. Kelly, A congruence theorem for trees (1957) = https://doi.org/10.2140/pjm.1957.7.961
-- L4DEL: source: J. A. Bondy and R. L. Hemminger, Graph reconstruction—a survey (1977) = https://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.3190010306
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = If DB(G) = DB(H), then G and H are bipartite-isomorphic.
-- L4DEL: quote: Paul J. Kelly, A congruence theorem for trees (1957) = Every type of proper subgraph which occurs in A or B
-- L4DEL: quote: J. A. Bondy and R. L. Hemminger, Graph reconstruction—a survey (1977) = every finite simple undirected graph on three or more vertices is determined, up to isomorphism, by its collection of vertex-deleted subgraphs

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl BipartiteIso
-- L4DEL: title: A graph isomorphism that preserves both sides of the bipartition
-- L4DEL: intuition: `BipartiteIso G H leftU rightV` strengthens an ordinary graph isomorphism by requiring its vertex equivalence to preserve membership in `leftU` and `rightV` separately. Swapping the two sides is not allowed.
-- L4DEL: step: The inherited relation isomorphism preserves and reflects adjacency.
-- L4DEL: step: `map_left` and `map_right` make preservation of the named parts explicit in both directions.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = is a graph isomorphism whose underlying bijection preserves both parts L and R.

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl bipartiteDeck
-- L4DEL: title: The multiset of incidence-deleted cards up to bipartite isomorphism
-- L4DEL: intuition: `bipartiteDeck G leftU rightV` records one card for every vertex `v`: all edges incident to `v` are deleted, `v` remains as an isolated vertex, and the result is stored as its entire bipartite-isomorphism class. Repeated isomorphism classes retain multiplicity.
-- L4DEL: step: `deleteIncidenceSet v` removes incident edges rather than deleting the vertex from the type.
-- L4DEL: step: Mapping over `univ` produces one set-valued card per vertex, and the outer `Multiset` forgets their labels while retaining how often each card occurs.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = The bipartite deck is the multiset

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl degreeProfile
-- L4DEL: title: The multiset of degrees seen around a vertex
-- L4DEL: intuition: `degreeProfile G x` lists `G.degree y` once for every neighbor `y` of `x`. It remembers the degrees surrounding `x` but deliberately forgets which neighboring vertex supplied each degree.
-- L4DEL: step: `neighborFinset x` supplies distinct adjacent vertices.
-- L4DEL: step: Mapping `G.degree` over its underlying multiset preserves multiplicity, which is essential when several neighbors have the same degree.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = The degree profile and type of x are

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl τ
-- L4DEL: title: A vertex type combines its degree with its neighbors' degrees
-- L4DEL: intuition: `τ G x` is the ordered pair consisting of the degree of `x` and its degree profile. Two vertices have the same type exactly when they have the same degree and the same multiset of neighboring degrees.
-- L4DEL: step: The first coordinate is local degree.
-- L4DEL: step: The second coordinate is a multiset, so it detects repeated neighbor degrees without identifying the neighbors themselves.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = The degree profile and type of x are

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl typeDrop
-- L4DEL: title: How a neighbor's type changes when one incident edge is removed
-- L4DEL: intuition: `typeDrop t d` lowers the type's degree coordinate by one using natural-number subtraction and erases one occurrence of `d` from its degree-profile multiset. It models deleting the edge to a vertex of degree `d`.
-- L4DEL: step: Only one degree occurrence is erased, matching one lost neighbor rather than all neighbors of that degree.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof applies this operation only to genuine neighbors, whose original degree is positive.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = d −̇ 1 = max(d−1, 0) is truncated subtraction and M \ {a} means deletion of one occurrence

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl multiset_map_eq_implies_bij
-- L4DEL: title: Equal unlabeled decks can be matched card by card
-- L4DEL: intuition: If two functions on the same finite type produce equal multisets of values, this lemma constructs a permutation pairing every input of the first function with an input of the second having the same value. Applied to decks, it recovers a bijection between the deleted vertices of matched cards.
-- L4DEL: step: Multiset equality supplies equal fiber cardinalities for every card value.
-- L4DEL: step: Those balanced fibers are assembled into an equivalence `e` satisfying `f a = g (e a)` for every input.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = Equality of the two decks also gives a bijection f : Ω → Ω

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl edgeCount_deleteIncidenceSet
-- L4DEL: title: An incidence-deleted card loses exactly the degree of its isolated vertex
-- L4DEL: intuition: Deleting every edge incident to `v` changes the edge count from `e(G)` to `e(G) − degree(v)`. Each incident undirected edge is removed once, even though degree is defined through the neighbor set.
-- L4DEL: step: The incidence set is identified with the image of the neighbors of `v` under the unordered-edge constructor.
-- L4DEL: step: Finite-set subtraction then gives the exact cardinality formula used to reconstruct degrees from card edge counts.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = removes exactly the edges incident to v

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl bipartiteDeck_determines_edgeCount
-- L4DEL: title: Recover the original number of edges from the deck
-- L4DEL: intuition: Equal bipartite decks of graphs on at least three vertices force equal total edge counts. Summing the edge counts of all cards counts each original edge once for every deleted vertex not incident to it, hence exactly `n−2` times.
-- L4DEL: step: Each card's edge count is invariant under the bipartite isomorphism class stored in the deck.
-- L4DEL: step: Positivity of `n−2` permits cancellation, turning equality of the card sums into `edgeCount G = edgeCount H`.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = equation (2) shows that the deck determines e(K)

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl degreeMultiset
-- L4DEL: title: The unlabeled multiset of all vertex degrees
-- L4DEL: intuition: `degreeMultiset G` lists the degree of every vertex of `G` while forgetting which vertex has which degree. This is the intermediate invariant reconstructed from the edge counts of the deck's cards.
-- L4DEL: step: Every vertex appears once through `Finset.univ`.
-- L4DEL: step: Using a multiset retains the multiplicity of each degree value.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = the degree multisets of G and H are equal

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl bipartiteDeck_determines_degreeMultiset
-- L4DEL: title: Equal decks determine the complete degree distribution
-- L4DEL: intuition: Once the original edge count and the multiset of card edge counts are known, the identity `degree(v)=e(G)−e(Δv(G))` recovers the multiset of vertex degrees. Equal decks therefore give equal degree multisets.
-- L4DEL: step: The deck first determines `edgeCount G = edgeCount H`.
-- L4DEL: step: Mapping subtraction by that common edge count over the matched card counts yields identical degree multisets.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Paul J. Kelly, A congruence theorem for trees (1957) = https://doi.org/10.2140/pjm.1957.7.961
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = equation (1) shows that the degree multisets of G and H are equal
-- L4DEL: quote: Paul J. Kelly, A congruence theorem for trees (1957) = Every type of proper subgraph which occurs in A or B

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl unique_isolated_of_2_connected
-- L4DEL: title: The deleted vertex is the unique isolated vertex of its card
-- L4DEL: intuition: Under the development's 2-connectivity hypothesis and `|V| ≥ 3`, the incidence-deleted card at `v` has degree zero exactly at `v`. Every other vertex retains an edge to someone other than `v`.
-- L4DEL: step: Connectivity rules out degree zero in the original graph.
-- L4DEL: step: Connectivity after deleting any one vertex rules out a vertex whose only neighbor was `v`, so no other isolated point is created.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = has a unique isolated vertex, namely v

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl H_is_2_connected
-- L4DEL: title: Matched incidence-deleted cards transfer 2-connectivity to H
-- L4DEL: intuition: If every incidence-deleted card of `G` is graph-isomorphic to a matched card of `H` and `G` is 2-connected, then every vertex-deleted induced subgraph of `H` is connected and hence `H` itself is connected.
-- L4DEL: step: The unique isolated vertex forces each card isomorphism to send the deleted vertex of `G` to its matched vertex of `H`.
-- L4DEL: step: Restricting the card isomorphism away from those isolated vertices transfers connectivity of every induced deletion; with at least three vertices, those connected deletions imply connectivity of `H`.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = Since the degree multisets of G and H are equal, H also has minimum degree at least 2.

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/bipartite_graph_reconstruction_conjecture_2.lean
@decl f_preserves_partitions
-- L4DEL: title: Card matching preserves the named left and right parts
-- L4DEL: intuition: The permutation matching deleted cards sends each vertex to a vertex in the same bipartition part. The key is that the deleted vertex is recognizable inside its card as the unique isolated vertex.
-- L4DEL: step: A bipartite card isomorphism preserves `leftU` and `rightV` by definition.
-- L4DEL: step: Uniqueness identifies its image of `v` with the matching permutation's `f v`, transferring both membership equivalences to `f`.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/graph_reconstruction.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written graph-reconstruction proof companion = Since φv preserves L and R, (6) implies that f preserves the two parts

