@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_12.parts.i.lean
@decl Erdos12.IsGood
-- L4DEL: title: Property P: no member divides the sum of two larger distinct members
-- L4DEL: intuition: `IsGood A` means that `A` is infinite and whenever `a,b,c ∈ A`, `a ∣ b+c`, `a<b`, and `a<c`, the two larger terms must coincide. Equivalently, no three distinct members realize the forbidden divisibility pattern.
-- L4DEL: step: The conclusion `b = c` is how the Lean predicate permits repeated use of one larger element while excluding a triple of distinct elements.
-- L4DEL: step: This is the formal version of the property introduced by Paul Erdős and András Sárközy; their ordered-sequence wording and the set-based Lean wording express the same restriction.
-- L4DEL: source: Paul Erdős and András Sárközy (1970), p. 97 = https://renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1970-13.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Paul Erdős and András Sárközy (1970), p. 97 = divides the sum of two larger terms

@decl Erdos12.q
-- L4DEL: title: A recursively chosen increasing sequence of primes
-- L4DEL: intuition: `q 0 = 5`; each `q (m+1)` is a prime chosen strictly above `q m` but no larger than `2*q m+2`.
-- L4DEL: step: `exists_prime_bounded` supplies the next prime using the Bertrand-type prime bound already available in Mathlib.
-- L4DEL: step: The lower bound gives new moduli for separating blocks, while the upper bound later controls their quantitative growth.

@decl Erdos12.K
-- L4DEL: title: The accumulated modulus from all earlier primes
-- L4DEL: intuition: `K 0 = 729 = 3^6`, and `K (m+1) = K m * q m`.
-- L4DEL: step: Thus `K m` is divisible by every earlier `q j` with `j<m`, as well as by `3`.
-- L4DEL: step: Members of block `m` will be congruent to `1` modulo `K m`, which makes them congruent to `1` modulo each earlier prime.

@decl Erdos12.P
-- L4DEL: title: The full period for block $m$
-- L4DEL: intuition: `P m = K m * q m`, combining the accumulated earlier-prime modulus with the block's new prime.
-- L4DEL: step: Because `K m` and `q m` are coprime, the Chinese remainder theorem selects a residue modulo this product satisfying both block congruences.
-- L4DEL: step: The arithmetic progression enumerating block elements advances by `P m`, so both congruences remain unchanged.

@decl Erdos12.M
-- L4DEL: title: The rapidly growing scale separating the blocks
-- L4DEL: intuition: `M m` is the eighth power of `P m`.
-- L4DEL: step: The eighth power makes room for roughly fourth-power-many progression points while keeping each block inside `[M m, 2*M m]`.
-- L4DEL: step: Since successive primes are at least five, the scales grow fast enough that one block ends before the next begins.

@decl Erdos12.MyGoodSet
-- L4DEL: title: The Lean proof's congruence-block witness
-- L4DEL: intuition: `MyGoodSet` contains a natural number `n` when some block index `m` places it near `M m`, below a controlled progression cutoff, with `n ≡ 1 (mod K m)` and `n ≡ 0 (mod q m)`.
-- L4DEL: step: The interval conditions are `M m ≤ n ≤ 2*M m` and a sharper upper bound determined by `P (m+1)^4/2` progression steps of size `P m`.
-- L4DEL: step: This is the witness actually formalized in Lean. The linked human-written companion proves the same final theorem with a different base-2-to-base-3 block construction.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 12(i) = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos12i.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 12(i) = We will construct a sequence of “blocks” that individually cannot contain such a, b, c

@decl Erdos12.M_increasing
-- L4DEL: title: Consecutive block scales are more than a factor of two apart
-- L4DEL: intuition: `M_increasing m` proves `2*M m < M (m+1)`.
-- L4DEL: step: The identity `M (m+1) = M m * q (m+1)^8` reduces the claim to the fact that the next prime is at least five.
-- L4DEL: step: This strict separation prevents the intervals `[M m,2*M m]` for different block indices from overlapping.

@decl Erdos12.m_le_of_lt
-- L4DEL: title: Numerical order respects block order
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `a` lies in block `ma`, `b` lies in block `mb`, and `a<b`, then `ma≤mb`.
-- L4DEL: step: If `mb<ma`, then the factor-of-two separation puts the start of block `ma` above the end of block `mb`, contradicting `a<b`.
-- L4DEL: step: The good-set proof uses this lemma to reduce all possible triples to later-block and same-block cases.

@decl Erdos12.mod_q_of_lt
-- L4DEL: title: A later block is $1$ modulo every earlier block prime
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `b ≡ 1 (mod K mb)` and `ma<mb`, then `b ≡ 1 (mod q ma)`.
-- L4DEL: step: `q_dvd_K` proves that the earlier prime `q ma` divides the later accumulated modulus `K mb`.
-- L4DEL: step: Reducing the block congruence through that divisibility transfers the residue `1` to the earlier prime.

@decl Erdos12.K_q_coprime
-- L4DEL: title: The accumulated modulus and the new prime are coprime
-- L4DEL: intuition: `K_q_coprime m` proves `Nat.Coprime (K m) (q m)`.
-- L4DEL: step: Every prime divisor of `K m` is smaller than `q m`, whereas `q m` itself is prime.
-- L4DEL: step: This coprimality is the exact hypothesis needed to combine residues `1 mod K m` and `0 mod q m` by the Chinese remainder theorem.

@decl Erdos12.r_val
-- L4DEL: title: A Chinese-remainder solution for the two block residues
-- L4DEL: intuition: `r_val m` selects the natural-number representative produced by the Chinese remainder theorem for residue `1` modulo `K m` and residue `0` modulo `q m`.
-- L4DEL: step: Existence depends on `K_q_coprime m`.
-- L4DEL: step: The definition is noncomputable because it chooses a representative from an existence theorem; the surrounding proof needs its specification, not an extraction algorithm.

@decl Erdos12.x_val
-- L4DEL: title: The canonical block offset modulo the full period
-- L4DEL: intuition: `x_val m` reduces `r_val m` modulo `P m = K m*q m`.
-- L4DEL: step: Consequently `x_val m < P m`, which keeps the first progression point close to the block's base scale.
-- L4DEL: step: Because both `K m` and `q m` divide `P m`, reduction modulo `P m` preserves the two Chinese-remainder congruences.

@decl Erdos12.x_val_mod_K
-- L4DEL: title: The canonical offset is $1$ modulo the accumulated modulus
-- L4DEL: intuition: `x_val_mod_K m` establishes `x_val m % K m = 1`.
-- L4DEL: step: It combines the Chinese-remainder specification of `r_val` with the fact that `K m ∣ P m`.
-- L4DEL: step: Adding multiples of `P m` therefore keeps every enumerated block element at residue one modulo `K m`.

@decl Erdos12.x_val_mod_q
-- L4DEL: title: The canonical offset is $0$ modulo the block's new prime
-- L4DEL: intuition: `x_val_mod_q m` establishes `x_val m % q m = 0`.
-- L4DEL: step: It transfers the second Chinese-remainder congruence through reduction modulo `P m`, using `q m ∣ P m`.
-- L4DEL: step: Every point in block `m` will consequently be divisible by `q m`.

@decl Erdos12.my_good_set_infinite
-- L4DEL: title: The congruence-block witness is infinite
-- L4DEL: intuition: Beyond every natural bound `N`, the proof constructs a member of `MyGoodSet` in block `N+1`.
-- L4DEL: step: `exists_good_in_block` supplies a number with the block inequalities and both required residues.
-- L4DEL: step: `M_gt_m` places that number above `N`, yielding the standard unboundedness characterization of an infinite set of naturals.

@decl Erdos12.my_good_set_is_good
-- L4DEL: title: The congruences exclude every forbidden divisibility triple
-- L4DEL: intuition: `my_good_set_is_good` proves that `MyGoodSet` is infinite and satisfies property P.
-- L4DEL: step: If one larger term lies in a later block, it is `1 mod q ma`; the other larger term is `0` or `1 mod q ma`, so their sum cannot be divisible by `q ma`, while `a` is divisible by that prime.
-- L4DEL: step: If all three terms lie in the same block, interval bounds force `(b+c)/a` to be `3` or `4`. Every block member is `1 mod 3`, but neither multiplier gives the required residue `2 mod 3` for `b+c`.
-- L4DEL: step: This is a complete congruence proof internal to the Lean file; it does not invoke the companion PDF's base-3 progression-free map.

@decl Erdos12.f_val
-- L4DEL: title: Enumerate the arithmetic progression inside one block
-- L4DEL: intuition: `f_val m i = M m + x_val m + i*P m` is the `i`th candidate in block `m`.
-- L4DEL: step: `M m` and `P m` are divisible by both `K m` and `q m`, while `x_val m` has residues one and zero respectively.
-- L4DEL: step: Varying `i` therefore produces distinct candidates with exactly the congruences required by `MyGoodSet`.

@decl Erdos12.q_pow_four_bound
-- L4DEL: title: The next prime grows slowly relative to the current period
-- L4DEL: intuition: The inequality `q (m+1)^4 ≤ P m^3` supplies the quantitative room needed to fit many progression points into block `m`.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof combines the recursive upper bound `q m + 2 ≤ 7*2^m` with the lower bound `3645*5^m ≤ P m`.
-- L4DEL: step: Raising those estimates to the relevant powers reduces the comparison to an explicit exponential inequality.

@decl Erdos12.f_val_in_good_set
-- L4DEL: title: Every bounded progression index produces a valid block member
-- L4DEL: intuition: For `i < P (m+1)^4/2`, the point `f_val m i` belongs to `MyGoodSet ∩ [M m,2*M m]`.
-- L4DEL: step: Growth estimates bound `i*P m` by `M m/2`, while `x_val m<P m≤M m/2`, placing the point inside the block interval and below the sharper cutoff.
-- L4DEL: step: Divisibility of `M m` and `P m` preserves `x_val`'s residues, proving the membership congruences.

@decl Erdos12.count_interval
-- L4DEL: title: Each block contains at least half a fourth power of points
-- L4DEL: intuition: Block `m` contains at least `P (m+1)^4/2` members of `MyGoodSet` between `M m` and `2*M m`.
-- L4DEL: step: The indices `0 ≤ i < P (m+1)^4/2` map into the block by `f_val_in_good_set`.
-- L4DEL: step: `f_val_inj` shows that different indices give different natural numbers, so the image cardinality supplies the lower bound.

@decl Erdos12.density_ineq
-- L4DEL: title: The next scale is controlled by the square of the block count
-- L4DEL: intuition: `density_ineq m` proves `2*M (m+1) ≤ 9*(P (m+1)^4/2)^2`.
-- L4DEL: step: Since `M (m+1)=P (m+1)^8`, the right-hand side is a constant multiple of the same eighth power after accounting for integer division by two.
-- L4DEL: step: This inequality converts the block cardinality lower bound into a uniform lower bound after division by `√N`.

@decl Erdos12.exists_M_interval
-- L4DEL: title: Locate every sufficiently large $N$ between consecutive block scales
-- L4DEL: intuition: For `N≥M 7`, there is an `m≥7` with `M m≤N<M (m+1)`.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof chooses the least index whose scale exceeds `N` and subtracts one.
-- L4DEL: step: Monotonicity and the initial lower bound ensure the preceding index exists and is at least seven.

@decl Erdos12.density_bound_N
-- L4DEL: title: A uniform lower bound for every sufficiently large cutoff
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every `N≥M 7`, the normalized count `|MyGoodSet∩[1,N]|/√N` is at least `1/3`.
-- L4DEL: step: Locate `N` between `M m` and `M (m+1)`. If `N<2*M m`, use the completed previous block; otherwise use the completed current block.
-- L4DEL: step: `count_interval` supplies the count in either case, and `density_ineq` bounds `N` by nine times the square of that count.
-- L4DEL: step: `density_ratio_bound` takes square roots and divides by the positive `√N` to obtain the constant `1/3`.

@decl Erdos12.g_val
-- L4DEL: title: Encode a good-set member by its block and progression cell
-- L4DEL: intuition: `g_val n` pairs the block index containing `n` with the quotient `(n-M k)/P k` inside that block.
-- L4DEL: step: `g_block` chooses the scale interval `M k≤n<M (k+1)`.
-- L4DEL: step: This coordinate map supports the separate upper bound needed to show the liminf is a finite real rather than positive infinity.

@decl Erdos12.g_val_inj
-- L4DEL: title: The block-and-cell coordinate is injective on the witness set
-- L4DEL: intuition: Two members of `MyGoodSet` with the same `g_val` must be the same natural number.
-- L4DEL: step: Equality of the first coordinate gives a common block; equality of the second gives a common quotient interval of width `P m`.
-- L4DEL: step: Matching residues modulo coprime `K m` and `q m` make their difference divisible by `P m`; being smaller than `P m` forces that difference to vanish.

@decl Erdos12.target_set
-- L4DEL: title: A finite coordinate box for all earlier blocks
-- L4DEL: intuition: `target_set m` contains pairs `(k,i)` with `k<m` and `0≤i≤P (k+1)^4/2`.
-- L4DEL: step: Every good-set member up to `M m` maps into this finite union of block-and-cell coordinates.
-- L4DEL: step: Its deliberately slightly generous inclusive cell range makes the containment proof simple while retaining the required fourth-power bound.

@decl Erdos12.count_upper_bound
-- L4DEL: title: The witness count at a block scale is at most twice a fourth power
-- L4DEL: intuition: Up to `M m`, `MyGoodSet` has at most `2*P m^4` members.
-- L4DEL: step: The injective map `g_val` sends those members into `target_set m`.
-- L4DEL: step: `target_set_card` bounds the finite coordinate set by summing the earlier fourth powers and comparing that sum with `2*P m^4`.

@decl Erdos12.exists_f_le_two
-- L4DEL: title: The normalized count is at most two infinitely often
-- L4DEL: intuition: Along the unbounded subsequence `N=M m`, the ratio `|MyGoodSet∩[1,N]|/√N` is at most `2`.
-- L4DEL: step: `count_upper_bound` gives the numerator bound `2*P m^4`.
-- L4DEL: step: Since `M m=P m^8`, its real square root is exactly `P m^4`, giving the ratio bound after cancellation.

@decl Erdos12.my_good_set_density
-- L4DEL: title: The normalized counting function has positive lower limit
-- L4DEL: intuition: The liminf of `|MyGoodSet∩[1,N]|/√N` is at least `1/3`, hence strictly positive.
-- L4DEL: step: `density_bound_N` supplies the eventual lower bound `1/3`.
-- L4DEL: step: `exists_f_le_two` supplies a frequently occurring upper bound, which proves the set of eventual lower bounds is bounded above and lets the proof use the real-valued `liminf` characterization safely.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof then applies `le_csSup` to obtain `1/3≤liminf` and concludes positivity.

@decl Erdos12.exists_prime_bounded
-- L4DEL: title: Find a controlled next prime
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every natural number `n`, there is a prime `p` with `n < p ≤ 2*n+2`.
-- L4DEL: step: The case `n=0` uses the prime `2`; otherwise Mathlib's Bertrand bound supplies a prime at most `2*n`.
-- L4DEL: step: This is the existence result used to define the prime sequence `q`.

@decl Erdos12.q_prime
-- L4DEL: title: Every chosen modulus is prime
-- L4DEL: intuition: Each value `q m` is prime.
-- L4DEL: step: The base value is `5`, and every successor is the witness selected by `exists_prime_bounded`.

@decl Erdos12.q_gt
-- L4DEL: title: Successive chosen primes strictly increase
-- L4DEL: intuition: `q m < q (m+1)` for every block index `m`.
-- L4DEL: step: This is the strict lower inequality carried by the witness selected in the recursive definition of `q`.

@decl Erdos12.q_ge_5
-- L4DEL: title: Every chosen prime is at least five
-- L4DEL: intuition: The increasing sequence `q` never falls below its initial value `5`.
-- L4DEL: step: Induction combines `q 0 = 5` with the strict growth lemma `q_gt`.

@decl Erdos12.K_pos
-- L4DEL: title: The accumulated modulus is positive
-- L4DEL: intuition: `K m` is positive for every block index.
-- L4DEL: step: `K 0=729`, and each successor multiplies a positive earlier value by the positive prime `q m`.

@decl Erdos12.P_pos
-- L4DEL: title: The joint period is positive
-- L4DEL: intuition: `P m = K m*q m` is always positive.
-- L4DEL: step: Positivity follows from `K_pos` and the lower bound `q m≥5`.

@decl Erdos12.M_succ
-- L4DEL: title: Successive block scales differ by an eighth power
-- L4DEL: intuition: `M (m+1) = M m * q (m+1)^8`.
-- L4DEL: step: Since `P (m+1)=P m*q (m+1)` and `M=P^8`, the identity follows by distributing the eighth power over multiplication.

@decl Erdos12.pow_eight_le
-- L4DEL: title: Eighth powers preserve order on naturals
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `a≤b`, then `a^8≤b^8`.
-- L4DEL: step: This elementary monotonicity lemma is used in the block-growth estimates.

@decl Erdos12.M_monotone
-- L4DEL: title: Block scales are monotone
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `m1≤m2`, then `M m1≤M m2`.
-- L4DEL: step: Induction advances through `M_increasing`, whose stronger conclusion says each next scale exceeds twice the previous one.

@decl Erdos12.q_dvd_K
-- L4DEL: title: Every earlier prime is absorbed into later accumulated moduli
-- L4DEL: intuition: When `ma<mb`, the prime `q ma` divides `K mb`.
-- L4DEL: step: The recursion `K (m+1)=K m*q m` inserts each chosen prime and preserves all earlier prime factors.

@decl Erdos12.q_div_a
-- L4DEL: title: Zero remainder gives divisibility by the block prime
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `a mod q ma = 0`, then `q ma` divides `a`.
-- L4DEL: step: This is the standard remainder characterization of divisibility.

@decl Erdos12.K_mod_3
-- L4DEL: title: Every accumulated modulus is divisible by three
-- L4DEL: intuition: `K m mod 3 = 0` for every `m`.
-- L4DEL: step: The base `K 0=729=3^6` is divisible by three, and multiplication by later primes preserves that factor.

@decl Erdos12.mod_3_eq_1
-- L4DEL: title: The block residue forces residue one modulo three
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `n mod K m = 1`, then `n mod 3 = 1`.
-- L4DEL: step: Because `3` divides `K m`, writing `n` as a multiple of `K m` plus one gives the claimed residue.

@decl Erdos12.prime_dvd_K
-- L4DEL: title: Prime factors of an accumulated modulus precede the current prime
-- L4DEL: intuition: If a prime `p` divides `K m`, then `p<q m`.
-- L4DEL: step: At `m=0`, every prime factor of `3^6` is at most `3<5`; the successor step splits a prime divisor of `K m*q m` between the two factors.
-- L4DEL: step: Strict growth of `q` then places either possible factor below `q (m+1)`.

@decl Erdos12.K_ge_3
-- L4DEL: title: The accumulated modulus is never smaller than three
-- L4DEL: intuition: `3≤K m` for every block index.
-- L4DEL: step: The base is `729`, and the recursion only multiplies by primes at least five.

@decl Erdos12.P_dvd_M
-- L4DEL: title: The joint period divides the block scale
-- L4DEL: intuition: `P m` divides `M m=P m^8`.
-- L4DEL: step: Factor the eighth power as `P m^7 * P m`.

@decl Erdos12.K_dvd_P
-- L4DEL: title: The accumulated modulus divides the joint period
-- L4DEL: intuition: `K m` divides `P m` because `P m=K m*q m`.

@decl Erdos12.q_dvd_P
-- L4DEL: title: The current prime divides the joint period
-- L4DEL: intuition: `q m` divides `P m` because `P m=K m*q m`.

@decl Erdos12.K_dvd_M
-- L4DEL: title: The accumulated modulus divides the block scale
-- L4DEL: intuition: `K m` divides `M m`.
-- L4DEL: step: Divisibility passes through `K m ∣ P m` and `P m ∣ M m`.

@decl Erdos12.q_dvd_M
-- L4DEL: title: The current prime divides the block scale
-- L4DEL: intuition: `q m` divides `M m`.
-- L4DEL: step: Divisibility passes through `q m ∣ P m` and `P m ∣ M m`.

@decl Erdos12.pow_four_le
-- L4DEL: title: Fourth powers preserve order on naturals
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `a≤b`, then `a^4≤b^4`.
-- L4DEL: step: This elementary monotonicity lemma supports the quantitative block estimates.

@decl Erdos12.x_val_lt_P
-- L4DEL: title: Choose the canonical residue below one period
-- L4DEL: intuition: `x_val m` is strictly smaller than `P m`.
-- L4DEL: step: By definition it is the remainder of `r_val m` modulo the positive number `P m`.

@decl Erdos12.exists_good_in_block
-- L4DEL: title: Every block contains at least one admissible residue
-- L4DEL: intuition: For each `m`, some `n` lies in the `m`th block and satisfies `n ≡ 1 (mod K m)` and `n ≡ 0 (mod q m)`.
-- L4DEL: step: The Chinese remainder theorem supplies a residue `x<P m` satisfying both congruences because `K m` and `q m` are coprime.
-- L4DEL: step: Taking `n=M m+x` preserves those residues because both moduli divide `M m`; the size estimates place `n` within the required block cutoff.

@decl Erdos12.M_gt_m
-- L4DEL: title: Block scales eventually dominate their indices immediately
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every index satisfies `m<M m`.
-- L4DEL: step: The base scale is positive, and `M_increasing` more than doubles the scale at each successor.

@decl Erdos12.P_le_M
-- L4DEL: title: One period fits inside its block scale
-- L4DEL: intuition: `P m≤M m=P m^8`.
-- L4DEL: step: Positivity of `P m` makes the seventh-power multiplier at least one.

@decl Erdos12.q_bound_le
-- L4DEL: title: An exponential upper bound for the chosen primes
-- L4DEL: intuition: `q m+2 ≤ 7*2^m`.
-- L4DEL: step: Each selected prime is at most `2*q m+2`, so the shifted quantity `q m+2` at most doubles at every step.

@decl Erdos12.P_lower_bound
-- L4DEL: title: An exponential lower bound for the joint periods
-- L4DEL: intuition: `3645*5^m ≤ P m`.
-- L4DEL: step: The base value is exactly `3645`, and every successor multiplies by a prime at least five.

@decl Erdos12.q_pow_four_bound_helper
-- L4DEL: title: Compare the explicit exponential envelopes
-- L4DEL: intuition: `38416*16^m ≤ 48427561125*125^m`.
-- L4DEL: step: The base case is numerical; induction preserves the inequality because the left side grows by `16` while the right side grows by `125`.

@decl Erdos12.f_val_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Progression offsets occupy at most half a block
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `i≤P (m+1)^4/2`, then `i*P m≤M m/2`.
-- L4DEL: step: Expand `P (m+1)^4` as `P m^4*q (m+1)^4` and use `q (m+1)^4≤P m^3`.
-- L4DEL: step: The resulting seventh power, after multiplying by `P m`, is exactly `M m=P m^8`.

@decl Erdos12.P_le_M_half
-- L4DEL: title: Even one full period fits inside half a block
-- L4DEL: intuition: `P m≤M m/2`.
-- L4DEL: step: Since `P m≥15`, its seventh power is at least two, so `2*P m≤P m^8=M m`.

@decl Erdos12.f_val_inj
-- L4DEL: title: Distinct progression indices give distinct block members
-- L4DEL: intuition: The map `i ↦ f_val m i` is injective on the permitted index interval.
-- L4DEL: step: Equality cancels the common block offset and leaves `i*P m=j*P m`; positivity of `P m` permits cancellation.

@decl Erdos12.finset_coe_ncard
-- L4DEL: title: Set cardinality agrees with finite-set cardinality
-- L4DEL: intuition: Viewing a finite set `s` as a `Set` does not change its number of elements.
-- L4DEL: step: This bridge lets the counting proof move between `Finset.card` and `Set.ncard`.

@decl Erdos12.good_set_inter_finite
-- L4DEL: title: A bounded slice of the witness set is finite
-- L4DEL: intuition: `MyGoodSet ∩ [M m,2*M m]` is finite.
-- L4DEL: step: It is a subset of the finite natural-number interval `[M m,2*M m]`.

@decl Erdos12.density_ineq_x
-- L4DEL: title: A floor-division estimate beyond ten
-- L4DEL: intuition: For `x≥10`, the inequality `8*x≤9*(x/2)^2` holds in natural arithmetic.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof controls the remainder modulo two and reduces the claim to a quadratic inequality in `x/2`.

@decl Erdos12.density_ineq_x_sq
-- L4DEL: title: A squared floor-division estimate beyond twenty
-- L4DEL: intuition: For `x≥20`, the inequality `2*x^2≤9*(x/2)^2` holds.
-- L4DEL: step: As in `density_ineq_x`, the proof accounts explicitly for the possible remainder when dividing by two.

@decl Erdos12.density_helper_pow
-- L4DEL: title: The density envelopes separate exponentially
-- L4DEL: intuition: `2151296*32^(m+6) ≤ 3375*125^(m+6)`.
-- L4DEL: step: The base inequality is checked directly, then induction uses the growth-rate comparison `32≤125`.

@decl Erdos12.density_ineq_q_helper
-- L4DEL: title: Compare prime-growth and period-growth envelopes
-- L4DEL: intuition: For `m≥6`, `4*(7*2^(m+1))^5 ≤ (15*5^m)^3`.
-- L4DEL: step: Writing `m=k+6` converts both sides to the exponential forms compared by `density_helper_pow`.

@decl Erdos12.density_ineq_q
-- L4DEL: title: Bound the fifth power of the next prime
-- L4DEL: intuition: For `m≥6`, `4*q (m+1)^5≤P m^3`.
-- L4DEL: step: The upper envelope for `q` and lower envelope for `P` reduce the claim to `density_ineq_q_helper`.

@decl Erdos12.density_ratio_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Convert a square count bound into a normalized density bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `C≤count` and `N≤9*C^2` with `N>0`, then `1/3≤count/√N`.
-- L4DEL: step: Taking square roots gives `√N≤3*C`; division by the positive `√N` yields the ratio bound.

@decl Erdos12.sum_P_pow_four
-- L4DEL: title: Earlier fourth-power periods are dominated by the latest one
-- L4DEL: intuition: `∑ k<m, P (k+1)^4 ≤ 2*P m^4`.
-- L4DEL: step: Each successor period is at least five times the preceding period, so its fourth power more than absorbs twice the preceding accumulated bound.

@decl Erdos12.dvd_sub_of_mod_eq
-- L4DEL: title: Equal residues make the difference divisible by the modulus
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `a` and `b` have the same remainder modulo `c` and `b≤a`, then `c` divides `a-b`.
-- L4DEL: step: Expanding both numbers by quotient and remainder cancels the common remainder and factors out `c`.

@decl Erdos12.div_eq_implies_sub_lt
-- L4DEL: title: Equal quotient cells are narrower than one period
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `a/P=b/P`, `b≤a`, and `P>0`, then `a-b<P`.
-- L4DEL: step: Both numbers share the same quotient, so their difference is controlled by remainders that are each smaller than `P`.

@decl Erdos12.good_set_inj
-- L4DEL: title: A block and quotient cell contain at most one admissible number
-- L4DEL: intuition: Two numbers with the block's required residues and the same quotient `(n-M m)/P m` must be equal.
-- L4DEL: step: Their difference is divisible by both coprime factors `K m` and `q m`, hence by `P m`.
-- L4DEL: step: The shared quotient cell makes that same difference smaller than `P m`; the only such multiple is zero.

@decl Erdos12.block_unique
-- L4DEL: title: A number has only one compatible block index
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `n∈[M m,2*M m]` and also `M k≤n<M (k+1)`, then `m=k`.
-- L4DEL: step: The separation `2*M j<M (j+1)` and monotonicity of `M` rule out either index being smaller than the other.

@decl Erdos12.g_block
-- L4DEL: title: Select the scale interval containing a number
-- L4DEL: intuition: `g_block n` is the least `k` with `M k≤n<M (k+1)`, when such a block exists; otherwise it is zero.
-- L4DEL: step: The definition uses `Nat.find`, so it is noncomputable but has a precise least-witness specification.

@decl Erdos12.g_block_spec
-- L4DEL: title: The selected block really contains the number
-- L4DEL: intuition: Whenever some scale interval contains `n`, the selected index satisfies `M (g_block n)≤n<M (g_block n+1)`.
-- L4DEL: step: Unfolding `g_block` reduces the statement to the witness specification of `Nat.find`.

@decl Erdos12.my_good_set_has_block
-- L4DEL: title: Every witness-set member lies between consecutive scales
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `n∈MyGoodSet`, then some `k` satisfies `M k≤n<M (k+1)`.
-- L4DEL: step: Membership already gives `n≤2*M k`, and block separation strengthens this to `n<M (k+1)`.

@decl Erdos12.target_set_card
-- L4DEL: title: Bound the finite code space for all earlier blocks
-- L4DEL: intuition: The encoding set `target_set m` has at most `2*P m^4` pairs.
-- L4DEL: step: Bound the union by the sum of its per-block index ranges, absorb the endpoint term into each fourth power, and apply `sum_P_pow_four`.

@decl Erdos12.g_val_maps_to
-- L4DEL: title: Every early witness-set member has a bounded block code
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `n∈MyGoodSet∩[1,M m]`, then `g_val n∈target_set m`.
-- L4DEL: step: The containing block must have index `k<m`; equality at `k=m` contradicts the required residue, and a later block starts above `M m`.
-- L4DEL: step: The block cutoff bounds the quotient coordinate by `P (k+1)^4/2`, placing the pair in the corresponding finite range.

@decl Erdos12.bdd_above_of_frequently_le
-- L4DEL: title: A frequent upper bound caps every eventual lower bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `f N≤c` frequently, then the set of real numbers that are eventually below `f N` is bounded above by `c`.
-- L4DEL: step: A frequent occurrence of `f N≤c` intersects any eventual assertion `a≤f N`, producing one index with `a≤c`.

@decl Erdos12.target_theorem_0
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős problem 12(i): a dense infinite set avoiding the divisibility pattern
-- L4DEL: intuition: There exists an infinite set `A` of natural numbers with no distinct `a,b,c` in `A` satisfying `a < b`, `a < c`, and `a ∣ b+c`, while `|A ∩ [1,N]|/√N` has positive lower limit.
-- L4DEL: step: The Lean proof witnesses the existential with `MyGoodSet`, whose prime and Chinese-remainder congruences establish `IsGood` and whose arithmetic-progression blocks establish an eventual normalized count of at least `1/3`.
-- L4DEL: step: The reverse implication is logically trivial because the left side is `answer(True)`; the mathematical content is the forward construction of the set.
-- L4DEL: step: Provenance boundary: the human-written companion proves the same theorem by a different construction using a base-2-to-base-3 map and progression-free blocks. Those proof details are not attributed to the Lean declarations above.
-- L4DEL: step: Erdős and Sárközy introduced property P and exhibited a weaker square-root-over-logarithm example in 1970; this theorem supplies the positive square-root-scale liminf asked for in problem 12(i).
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 12(i) = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos12i.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Erdős Problems 12 source record = https://www.erdosproblems.com/12
-- L4DEL: source: Paul Erdős and András Sárközy (1970), p. 97 = https://renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1970-13.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 12(i) = We will construct a sequence of “blocks” that individually cannot contain such a, b, c
-- L4DEL: quote: Paul Erdős and András Sárközy (1970), p. 97 = divides the sum of two larger terms

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_12.parts.ii.lean
@decl Erdos12.target_theorem_0
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős problem 12(ii): no universal power saving for every admissible set
-- L4DEL: intuition: The proposed universal exponent `c > 0` is false: one can construct an admissible infinite set whose counting function is at least `N^(1-c)` eventually for every chosen positive `c`. The theorem packages that negative answer as the failure of the asserted uniform sparse-subsequence bound.
-- L4DEL: step: A Behrend-style sphere supplies dense finite sets with no three-term arithmetic progression.
-- L4DEL: step: Chinese-remainder conditions place those sets in separated blocks while preventing the forbidden divisibility relation across blocks.
-- L4DEL: step: The blocks are large enough that the union eventually exceeds every fixed `N^(1-c)` threshold.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 12(ii) = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos12ii.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Erdős Problems 12 source record = https://www.erdosproblems.com/12
-- L4DEL: source: Felix Behrend (1946), original PNAS paper, p. 331 = https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.32.12.331
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 12(ii) = The proof is similar to the previous one for Erdős #12(i), differing mainly in the use of a Behrend-style construction
-- L4DEL: quote: Felix Behrend (1946), original PNAS paper, p. 331 = Let S be a set of non-negative integers < N no three of which form an arithmetical progression

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_125.variants.positive_lower_density.lean
@decl Erdos125.zero_in_A
-- L4DEL: title: Zero belongs to the base-3 digit set
-- L4DEL: intuition: The natural number `0` belongs to `A` because its base-3 expansion contains no digit outside `{0,1}`.
-- L4DEL: step: This supplies the additive identity used to place zero in the sumset.

@decl Erdos125.zero_in_B
-- L4DEL: title: Zero belongs to the base-4 digit set
-- L4DEL: intuition: The natural number `0` belongs to `B` because its base-4 expansion contains no digit outside `{0,1}`.
-- L4DEL: step: Together with `zero_in_A`, this gives a concrete representation of zero in `A+B`.

@decl Erdos125.zero_in_A_plus_B
-- L4DEL: title: The sumset contains zero
-- L4DEL: intuition: Since `0∈A` and `0∈B`, the equality `0=0+0` witnesses `0∈A+B`.
-- L4DEL: step: This fact is used when the final lower-density argument needs a nonempty filtered interval.

@decl Erdos125.A_max_k
-- L4DEL: title: The largest k-digit base-3 number using only zero and one
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `x∈A` and `x<3^k`, then `x≤(3^k-1)/2`, the number whose first `k` base-3 digits are all one.
-- L4DEL: step: The geometric sum `1+3+⋯+3^(k-1)` equals `(3^k-1)/2`.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof inducts over digit positions and uses membership in `A` to exclude a base-3 digit equal to two.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos125.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = the maximum possible value for abot is the integer consisting of k ones in base 3

@decl Erdos125.B_max_m
-- L4DEL: title: The largest m-digit base-4 number using only zero and one
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `y∈B` and `y<4^m`, then `y≤(4^m-1)/3`, the number whose first `m` base-4 digits are all one.
-- L4DEL: step: The geometric sum `1+4+⋯+4^(m-1)` equals `(4^m-1)/3`.
-- L4DEL: step: The digit restriction eliminates residues two and three at every base-4 position.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos125.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = yielding the analogous bound

@decl Erdos125.A_B_gap
-- L4DEL: title: A common digit-scale gap in the sumset
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `x` is below both `3^k` and `4^m` but exceeds `(3^k-1)/2+(4^m-1)/3`, then `x∉A+B`.
-- L4DEL: step: Any representation `x=a+b` forces `a<3^k` and `b<4^m`; `A_max_k` and `B_max_m` then make their sum too small.
-- L4DEL: step: This lemma isolates the gap mechanism, while the stronger density proof below tracks top digits as well as the gap.

@decl Erdos125.A_decomp
-- L4DEL: title: Split a base-3 digit string into top and bottom blocks
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every `a∈A` can be written `a=a1*3^k+a0`, where both `a1` and `a0` remain in `A` and `a0<3^k`.
-- L4DEL: step: Choose `a1=a/3^k` and `a0=a mod 3^k`.
-- L4DEL: step: Division discards the first `k` digits and remainder keeps exactly those digits, so neither operation introduces a digit outside `{0,1}`.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos125.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = we can decompose it at the k-th digit into a top and bottom part

@decl Erdos125.B_decomp
-- L4DEL: title: Split a base-4 digit string into top and bottom blocks
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every `b∈B` can be written `b=b1*4^m+b0`, where both pieces remain in `B` and `b0<4^m`.
-- L4DEL: step: The quotient `b/4^m` retains the top digits and the remainder `b mod 4^m` retains the bottom `m` digits.
-- L4DEL: step: Because the original digits are only zero or one, both resulting digit strings satisfy the same restriction.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos125.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = applying the exact same logic to any b ∈ B at scale m

@decl Erdos125.log_ratio_irrational
-- L4DEL: title: The logarithms of four and three have irrational ratio
-- L4DEL: intuition: `log 4 / log 3` is irrational; otherwise a rational relation would force a nontrivial equality between a power of three and a power of four.
-- L4DEL: step: Clearing a hypothetical rational denominator and exponentiating gives `3^u=4^v` for positive integers.
-- L4DEL: step: The left side is odd and the right side is even, so the equality is impossible.

@decl Erdos125.exists_small_pos_lin_comb_help
-- L4DEL: title: A positive irrational has arbitrarily small positive integer linear combinations
-- L4DEL: intuition: For positive irrational `α` and every `δ>0`, there are positive naturals `m,k` with `0<m*α-k<δ`.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof starts from infinitely many rational approximations to `α` and handles approximants on either side so the final error is positive.
-- L4DEL: step: Scaling or reflecting a sufficiently accurate approximation produces the required positive integer pair.

@decl Erdos125.exists_small_pos_lin_comb
-- L4DEL: title: Make m log 4 minus k log 3 positive and arbitrarily small
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every `δ>0`, positive naturals `m,k` satisfy `0<m*log 4-k*log 3<δ`.
-- L4DEL: step: Apply `exists_small_pos_lin_comb_help` to `α=log 4/log 3`, then multiply through by the positive number `log 3`.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos125.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = Because the ratio of logarithms ln(4)/ ln(3) is irrational

@decl Erdos125.exists_small_pos_lin_comb_large_k
-- L4DEL: title: Obtain the logarithmic approximation beyond any requested scale
-- L4DEL: intuition: The exponents in the small positive logarithmic combination can also be chosen so that `3^k` exceeds any real threshold `K`.
-- L4DEL: step: Choose a positive multiplier `N` with `3^N≥K`, find an error below `δ/N`, and multiply both exponents by `N`.
-- L4DEL: step: Scaling preserves positivity, keeps the total error below `δ`, and makes the base-3 power large enough.

@decl Erdos125.dirichlet_approx
-- L4DEL: title: Choose nearby powers of three and four at a large scale
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every `ε>0`, positive `k,m` can be chosen with `3^k≤4^m≤3^k*(1+ε)` and `3^k*ε≥3`.
-- L4DEL: step: Make `m*log 4-k*log 3` lie between zero and `log(1+ε)`, then exponentiate both inequalities.
-- L4DEL: step: The large-scale refinement ensures the additive error budget used in the counting step is at least three.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos125.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = Dirichlet’s Approximation Theorem guarantees that we can find arbitrarily large integers k and m

@decl Erdos125.hz_eq_lemma
-- L4DEL: title: Rewrite a sum using the smaller common scale
-- L4DEL: intuition: When `3^k≤4^m`, decompositions of `a` and `b` give `x=(a1+b1)*3^k+z`, with `z=a0+b0+b1*(4^m-3^k)`.
-- L4DEL: step: Replace `4^m` by `3^k+(4^m-3^k)` and collect the two top-digit terms multiplying `3^k`.
-- L4DEL: step: The remainder records both bottom digit blocks and the small mismatch between the nearby powers.

@decl Erdos125.scale_step
-- L4DEL: title: Reduce density by a fixed factor at a larger scale
-- L4DEL: intuition: If the count below `N` is at most `C*N`, then at a suitable scale `N'=N*3^k` the count is at most `(11/12)*C*N'`.
-- L4DEL: step: Choose `3^k` and `4^m` within relative error `ε≤1/(24N)` and split every sum below `N*3^k` into a top sum `y<N` and a bounded remainder `z`.
-- L4DEL: step: The top coordinate has at most `C*N` choices; digit maxima and the power mismatch give fewer than `3^k*(5/6+2εN)` remainder choices.
-- L4DEL: step: Since `5/6+2εN≤11/12`, multiplying the two coordinate counts produces the uniform density contraction.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos125.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = the density up to M · N0 is less than or equal to the density up to N0
-- L4DEL: source: GTSoukalas, Erdős Problems 125 discussion, 30 March 2026 = https://www.erdosproblems.com/forum/thread/125
-- L4DEL: quote: GTSoukalas, Erdős Problems 125 discussion, 30 March 2026 = We want to go from density <δ at scale N to density <cδ at some bigger scale MN

@decl Erdos125.density_multi_scale
-- L4DEL: title: Iterate the contraction through arbitrarily many scales
-- L4DEL: intuition: After `d` scale steps, some positive cutoff `N` has normalized count at most `(11/12)^d`.
-- L4DEL: step: The base case uses `N=1`; the successor case feeds the current bound into `scale_step`.
-- L4DEL: step: The new factor multiplies the previous geometric bound, yielding the next power of `11/12`.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos125.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = After r iterations, the density of the set up to the scale Nr is bounded by (0.99)r

@decl Erdos125.limit_11_12
-- L4DEL: title: Powers of eleven twelfths fall below every positive threshold
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every `ε>0`, some natural exponent `d` satisfies `(11/12)^d≤ε`.
-- L4DEL: step: This is geometric decay for a real base strictly between zero and one.

@decl Erdos125.density_tends_to_zero
-- L4DEL: title: Find a cutoff with density below any positive epsilon
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every `ε>0`, there is a positive `N` with `|(A+B)∩[0,N)|≤εN`.
-- L4DEL: step: Choose `d` so `(11/12)^d≤ε`, then use the cutoff supplied by `density_multi_scale d`.
-- L4DEL: step: This is the quantified subsequence estimate needed to force the lower density to zero.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos125.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = proving that the lower density of A + B is 0

@decl Erdos125.target_theorem_0
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős problem 125: the base-3 plus base-4 digit sumset has zero lower density
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let `A` contain the nonnegative integers using only digits 0 and 1 in base 3, and `B` the analogous set in base 4. Their sumset does not have positive lower density: along suitable scales its proportion can be made arbitrarily small.
-- L4DEL: step: Split base-3 and base-4 expansions into top and bottom digit blocks at independently chosen scales.
-- L4DEL: step: Irrationality of `log 4/log 3` supplies arbitrarily close powers `3^k` and `4^m`, so the number of possible remainders grows by a factor strictly below the ambient scale.
-- L4DEL: step: The Lean proof uses the explicit contraction `11/12` rather than the companion's expository `0.99`; either fixed factor below one yields geometric decay.
-- L4DEL: step: `density_tends_to_zero` is converted to `(A+B).lowerDensity=0`, making both sides of `answer(False) ↔ 0<lowerDensity` false.
-- L4DEL: step: Historical provenance: Burr, Erdős, Graham, and Li asked the density question in 1996. The current Erdős Problems record explains why the formal target adopts the lower-density interpretation.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos125.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Erdős Problems 125 source record = https://www.erdosproblems.com/125
-- L4DEL: source: Burr, Erdős, Graham, and Li (1996), p. 137 = https://doi.org/10.4064/aa-77-2-133-138
-- L4DEL: source: Erdős Problems 125 discussion (Giuseppe Melfi and contributors) = https://www.erdosproblems.com/forum/thread/125
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 125 = We show that the answer is no, the lower density is zero.
-- L4DEL: quote: Burr, Erdős, Graham, and Li (1996), p. 137 = have positive density? positive upper density? For example, what about set {3, 4}?
-- L4DEL: quote: Erdős Problems 125 discussion (Giuseppe Melfi and contributors) = I was surprised to discover that the lower asymptotic density is zero

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.monoAP_guarantee_set
-- L4DEL: title: Interval lengths that force a monochromatic arithmetic progression
-- L4DEL: intuition: `monoAP_guarantee_set r k` contains exactly those natural numbers `N` for which every `r`-colouring of `{1,...,N}` contains a monochromatic arithmetic progression of length `k`.
-- L4DEL: step: Membership is the forcing property itself; it does not yet choose the least such interval length.
-- L4DEL: step: The colouring is represented as a function from `Finset.Icc 1 N` to `Fin r`, so the domain and the number of colours are encoded in its type.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 162 = https://doi.org/10.1007/s40598-018-0090-5
-- L4DEL: quote: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 162 = contains a monochromatic l-term arithmetic progression.

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.monoAPNumber
-- L4DEL: title: The least interval length that forces a monochromatic progression
-- L4DEL: intuition: `monoAPNumber r k` is the infimum of the forcing set, hence the formal van der Waerden number for `r` colours and progression length `k` once that set is known to be nonempty.
-- L4DEL: step: The definition is noncomputable: it specifies the least forcing length mathematically rather than giving an algorithm for calculating it.
-- L4DEL: step: Nonemptiness is not hidden in this definition; `W_is_nonempty` proves it later from van der Waerden's theorem through an ultrafilter compactness argument.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 163 = https://doi.org/10.1007/s40598-018-0090-5
-- L4DEL: quote: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 163 = The smallest N guaranteed by the theorem is often denoted by W(l, k) and called a van der Waerden number.

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.W
-- L4DEL: title: The two-colour van der Waerden number `W(k)`
-- L4DEL: intuition: `W` abbreviates `monoAPNumber 2`, so `W k` is the least interval length that forces a monochromatic `k`-term arithmetic progression under every red-blue colouring.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an abbreviation, not a new theorem: Lean unfolds it to the two-colour specialization of `monoAPNumber` whenever needed.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 163 = https://doi.org/10.1007/s40598-018-0090-5

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.HasMonoAP
-- L4DEL: title: An explicit monochromatic arithmetic progression inside `[1,N]`
-- L4DEL: intuition: `HasMonoAP c N k` supplies a first term `a` and positive step `d` such that all `k` terms `a+m*d` have colour `c a`, start at or above 1, and end at or before `N`.
-- L4DEL: step: The endpoint bound is written `a+(k-1)*d≤N`; the colour condition quantifies over every index `m<k`.
-- L4DEL: step: This whole-number formulation is convenient for extending a colouring one integer at a time; two later bridge lemmas connect it to Mathlib's set-valued `ContainsMonoAPofLength` predicate.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 163 = https://doi.org/10.1007/s40598-018-0090-5
-- L4DEL: quote: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 163 = An l-term arithmetic progression is a set of the form {a, a + d,..., a + (l − 1)d}.

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.ap_must_end
-- L4DEL: title: Any newly created `(k+1)`-progression must end at the new point
-- L4DEL: intuition: If the old colouring has no `(k+1)`-term progression through `N+i`, but assigning a colour at `N+i+1` creates one, that progression must use `N+i+1` as its last term.
-- L4DEL: step: If its endpoint were at most `N+i`, every one of its terms would retain the old colour, contradicting the hypothesis that the old interval had no such progression.
-- L4DEL: step: Removing the final term leaves `k` old points of the newly assigned colour, a structure used to compare the two blocked colour choices.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos138.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 138 = the (k + 1)-th element for both progressions lands exactly on M + 1

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.extend_one_step
-- L4DEL: title: At least one colour safely extends the progression-free interval
-- L4DEL: intuition: While fewer than `k` new points have been appended, a two-colouring with no old `k`-progression and no current `(k+1)`-progression can always colour the next point without creating a `(k+1)`-progression.
-- L4DEL: step: If both colours were blocked, `ap_must_end` would produce a red and a blue `k`-term prefix ending at the same new point.
-- L4DEL: step: Each step size is at most `i`; a larger step would put the entire `k`-term prefix inside the original interval and contradict the absence of an old `k`-progression.
-- L4DEL: step: Stepping back `d₁` places along the colour-0 progression and `d₀` places along the colour-1 progression reaches the same integer `N+i+1-d₀d₁`, forcing it to have both colours.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos138.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: George Tsoukalas et al., AlphaProof Nexus paper, Erdős 138 appendix = https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22763v2
-- L4DEL: source: Erdős Problems 138 discussion (Adam Zsolt Wagner and Thomas Bloom) = https://www.erdosproblems.com/forum/thread/138
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 138 = both calculations land exactly on the positive integer M + 1 − dR dB
-- L4DEL: quote: George Tsoukalas et al., AlphaProof Nexus paper, Erdős 138 appendix = The only way this algorithm could result in an invalid coloring is if both choices are blocked
-- L4DEL: quote: Erdős Problems 138 discussion (Adam Zsolt Wagner and Thomas Bloom) = any two arithmetic progressions of length k and step size <k with the same start/end point must have at least one other point in common

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.has_mono_ap_ext
-- L4DEL: title: The progression predicate depends only on colours inside `[1,N]`
-- L4DEL: intuition: If two total colourings agree at every positive integer at most `N`, then either both contain a monochromatic `k`-term progression inside that interval or neither does.
-- L4DEL: step: The endpoint inequality places the first term and every indexed term within the agreement range, so the same witnesses `a,d` transfer in both directions.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 162 = https://doi.org/10.1007/s40598-018-0090-5
-- L4DEL: quote: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 162 = a k-colouring of a set A is any function c : A → B

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.extend_j_steps
-- L4DEL: title: Iterating the safe extension for up to `k` new points
-- L4DEL: intuition: From a colouring of `[1,N]` with no monochromatic `k`-term progression, the lemma constructs a colouring through `N+j`, for every `j≤k`, with no monochromatic `(k+1)`-term progression and with all original colours preserved.
-- L4DEL: step: At `j=0`, any `(k+1)`-progression would already contain a forbidden `k`-term prefix.
-- L4DEL: step: The successor case first transfers the old `k`-progression-free property to the current extension, then invokes `extend_one_step` and proves the new assignment does not alter positions at most `N`.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos138.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 138 = we can extend it by k further elements without creating a monochromatic (k + 1)-AP

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.extend_coloring
-- L4DEL: title: Extend a finite interval colouring to every natural number
-- L4DEL: intuition: `extend_coloring N c` agrees with the subtype-valued colouring `c` on `{1,...,N}` and assigns colour `0` everywhere outside that interval.
-- L4DEL: step: The arbitrary outside value is bookkeeping only: every later use proves that the progression under discussion lies in the original interval.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 162 = https://doi.org/10.1007/s40598-018-0090-5

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.ap_equiv
-- L4DEL: title: Indexing a positive-step arithmetic progression by `Fin k`
-- L4DEL: intuition: For `d>0`, the map `m ↦ a+m*d` is an equivalence from the `k` indices to the set of the first `k` terms of the arithmetic progression.
-- L4DEL: step: The inverse recovers the index as `(x-a)/d`; positivity of `d` makes multiplication injective and validates the division identities.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 163 = https://doi.org/10.1007/s40598-018-0090-5

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.card_ap_eq_k
-- L4DEL: title: A positive-step `k`-term progression has exactly `k` elements
-- L4DEL: intuition: The underlying set `{a+m*d | m<k}` has extended-natural cardinality `k` whenever `d>0` and `k>0`.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof transports the known cardinality of `Fin k` across `ap_equiv`; no separate counting or no-duplication argument is needed.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.card_ap_pos_d
-- L4DEL: title: More than one progression term forces a positive step
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `k>1` and the set `{a+m*d | m<k}` really has cardinality `k`, then `d` cannot be zero.
-- L4DEL: step: With `d=0` every indexed term collapses to `a`, making the set a singleton and contradicting `k>1`.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 163 = https://doi.org/10.1007/s40598-018-0090-5

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.contains_mono_ap_imp
-- L4DEL: title: Convert Mathlib's set-valued progression witness to `HasMonoAP`
-- L4DEL: intuition: A monochromatic arithmetic-progression subset of `Finset.Icc 1 N` yields explicit natural-number witnesses `a,d` satisfying `HasMonoAP` for the extended colouring.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof identifies the coerced subtype set with `{a+m*d | m<k}` and transports its cardinality.
-- L4DEL: step: For `k=1` it may choose step `1`; for `k>1`, `card_ap_pos_d` recovers the strictly positive step required by `HasMonoAP`.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.imp_contains_mono_ap
-- L4DEL: title: Convert `HasMonoAP` back to Mathlib's interval predicate
-- L4DEL: intuition: Explicit witnesses `a,d` for a monochromatic progression inside `[1,N]` define the required progression subset of the interval subtype.
-- L4DEL: step: The endpoint bound proves every term lies in `Finset.Icc 1 N`; `card_ap_eq_k` supplies the exact size, and the original colour equation supplies monochromaticity.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.not_guarantee_extend
-- L4DEL: title: A countercolouring for `k` extends to one for `k+1`
-- L4DEL: intuition: If some two-colouring of `{1,...,N}` avoids every `k`-term progression, then some two-colouring of `{1,...,N+k}` avoids every `(k+1)`-term progression.
-- L4DEL: step: Extend the finite colouring to all naturals, apply `extend_j_steps` with `j=k`, and translate the resulting explicit predicate back to `ContainsMonoAPofLength` only to contradict it.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
