-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 138 = Given a 2-coloring of the first W(k) − 1 integers without a monochromatic k-AP

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.not_in_set_of_lt_sInf
-- L4DEL: title: Nothing below a natural-number infimum belongs to the set
-- L4DEL: intuition: For any set of naturals `s`, if `n<sInf s`, then `n∉s`.
-- L4DEL: step: Membership would imply `sInf s≤n` by the defining lower-bound property of the infimum, contradicting the strict inequality.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.U_limit_color
-- L4DEL: title: The ultrafilter-limit colour at one integer
-- L4DEL: intuition: Given a sequence of two-colourings `C n`, `U_limit_color C U x` chooses colour `0` when the indices assigning `0` to `x` are large in `U`, and otherwise chooses colour `1`.
-- L4DEL: step: This is a proof-oriented compactness construction in the Lean file, not a step stated in the one-page human-written companion.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean#L607-L608

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.U_limit_color_mem
-- L4DEL: title: The chosen limit colour occurs on a `U`-large set of indices
-- L4DEL: intuition: For each position `x`, the indices `n` satisfying `C n x = U_limit_color C U x` belong to the ultrafilter.
-- L4DEL: step: If the colour-0 fibre is not in `U`, ultrafilter maximality puts its complement in `U`; because `Fin 2` has only two values, that complement is exactly the colour-1 fibre.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean#L610-L643

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.U_inter_mem
-- L4DEL: title: Finitely many progression positions agree with the limit colouring together
-- L4DEL: intuition: For fixed `a,b,k`, the indices whose colourings agree with the ultrafilter-limit colouring at every point `a*s+b` for `s<k` form a set in `U`.
-- L4DEL: step: Induction on `k` reduces the claim to closure of a filter under finite intersections.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean#L645-L674

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.U_atTop_mem_large
-- L4DEL: title: A `U`-large set contains arbitrarily large indices
-- L4DEL: intuition: When the ultrafilter lies above `atTop`, every set in it meets every tail `{n | n≥M}`.
-- L4DEL: step: Intersect the given set with the tail inside `U`; a member of an ultrafilter is nonempty, so the intersection supplies an index at least `M`.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean#L676-L683

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.W_is_nonempty
-- L4DEL: title: Van der Waerden forcing lengths exist for every progression length
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every `k`, some finite `N` forces a monochromatic `k`-term progression under every two-colouring, so the set whose infimum defines `W k` is nonempty.
-- L4DEL: step: For `k=0` the empty progression is immediate. Otherwise, assume countercolourings exist for every finite `N`, extend them to all naturals, and take an ultrafilter-limit colouring.
-- L4DEL: step: Mathlib's infinite van der Waerden theorem gives a monochromatic homothetic copy in the limit colouring. Finite intersection and an arbitrarily large matching index transfer that progression back to one of the alleged countercolourings.
-- L4DEL: step: Blondal and Jungić describe van der Waerden's original double-induction proof of existence; the Lean declaration instead uses this ultrafilter compactness route. The sources support the theorem, not an identity of proofs.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean#L685-L785
-- L4DEL: source: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 163 = https://doi.org/10.1007/s40598-018-0090-5
-- L4DEL: source: Paul Erdős, On the combinatorial problems which I would most like to see solved (1981), p. 5 = https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02579174
-- L4DEL: quote: Blondal and Jungić, Proof of van der Waerden's Theorem in Nine Figures (2018), p. 163 = follows van der Waerden’s original idea to establish the existence of W(l, k) by using double induction.
-- L4DEL: quote: Paul Erdős, On the combinatorial problems which I would most like to see solved (1981), p. 5 = proved that there is a smallest integer f (n)

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.Icc_subset_succ
-- L4DEL: title: The interval `[1,N]` sits inside `[1,N+1]`
-- L4DEL: intuition: Any natural number between `1` and `N` is also between `1` and `N+1`.
-- L4DEL: step: This small coercion lemma lets the proof restrict a colouring of the larger interval to the smaller subtype without changing its values.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean#L787-L789

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.guarantee_upward_closed
-- L4DEL: title: Once an interval length guarantees a progression, every larger one does
-- L4DEL: intuition: If every `r`-colouring of `{1,...,N}` contains a monochromatic `k`-term progression, then the same is true for `{1,...,N+1}`.
-- L4DEL: step: Restrict an arbitrary colouring of the larger interval to the first `N` points, obtain the progression there, and lift its subtype-valued set back into the larger interval.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean#L791-L839

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.not_in_guarantee_lt_sInf
-- L4DEL: title: A non-guaranteeing length lies below the van der Waerden number
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `N` does not force a two-colour `k`-term progression, then `N<W k`.
-- L4DEL: step: `W_is_nonempty` makes the infimum a genuine minimum, while `guarantee_upward_closed` shows every number at or above that minimum belongs to the forcing set.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Nexus Lean proof for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean#L841-L857
