@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_138.variants.difference.lean
@decl Erdos138.target_theorem_0
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős problem 138: consecutive van der Waerden numbers separate without bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: For the two-colour van der Waerden number `W(k)`, the difference `W(k+1)-W(k)` tends to infinity. The proof establishes the explicit lower bound `W(k+1) ≥ W(k)+k`, which immediately forces the differences upward.
-- L4DEL: step: Begin with a two-colouring of `[1,W(k)-1]` containing no monochromatic `k`-term arithmetic progression.
-- L4DEL: step: Greedily colour `k` more integers; if both colours were blocked at one step, two terminal progressions would force one earlier point to have both colours.
-- L4DEL: step: Hence a colouring without a monochromatic `(k+1)`-term progression exists through `W(k)+k-1`, proving the bound.
-- L4DEL: step: The Lean file separately proves that every `W(k)` exists, using an ultrafilter limit and Mathlib's infinite van der Waerden theorem; the one-page companion states the greedy difference argument rather than that compactness implementation.
-- L4DEL: step: Erdős's 1981 source asks both a quotient question and this weaker difference question. This theorem resolves only the difference variant; it does not claim that `W(k+1)/W(k)→∞` or settle the stronger growth problem.
-- L4DEL: step: Formally the statement is `answer(True) ↔ Tendsto ...`; the reverse implication is trivial because the recorded answer is already `True`, while the forward implication uses the quantitative bound.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 138 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos138.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Erdős Problems 138 source record = https://www.erdosproblems.com/138
-- L4DEL: source: George Tsoukalas et al., AlphaProof Nexus paper, Erdős 138 appendix = https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22763v2
-- L4DEL: source: Paul Erdős, On the combinatorial problems which I would most like to see solved (1981), p. 6 = https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02579174
-- L4DEL: source: Bartel van der Waerden's 1971 recollection, reproduced by Blondal and Jungić (2018), p. 162 = https://doi.org/10.1007/s40598-018-0090-5
-- L4DEL: source: Erdős Problems 138 discussion (Adam Zsolt Wagner and Thomas Bloom) = https://www.erdosproblems.com/forum/thread/138
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 138 = We will show that W(k + 1) ≥ W(k) + k
-- L4DEL: quote: George Tsoukalas et al., AlphaProof Nexus paper, Erdős 138 appendix = We will show that W(k + 1) ≥ W(k) + k
-- L4DEL: quote: Paul Erdős, On the combinatorial problems which I would most like to see solved (1981), p. 6 = Can one at least prove that f(n + 1) − f(n) → ∞?
-- L4DEL: quote: Bartel van der Waerden's 1971 recollection, reproduced by Blondal and Jungić (2018), p. 162 = Whatever one makes explicit and draws is much easier to remember and to reproduce than mere thoughts
-- L4DEL: quote: Erdős Problems 138 discussion (Adam Zsolt Wagner and Thomas Bloom) = The argument is rather simple, once one knows what the right thing to prove is.

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_152.lean
@decl Erdos152.target_theorem_0
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős problem 152: Sidon sumsets have quadratically many isolated sums
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `f(n)` is the minimum number of isolated elements in `A+A` over Sidon sets `A` of size `n`, then `f(n)` tends to infinity. In fact the formal proof derives the quantitative lower bound `(n²-100n-16)/16`.
-- L4DEL: step: Count neighbour pairs at distances 1, 2, and 3 in the sumset and relate them to isolated and interior points.
-- L4DEL: step: Use the Sidon property to control how often additive representations and short differences can coincide.
-- L4DEL: step: Combining the counting inequalities yields `16·I(A+A)+100n+16 ≥ n²`, and the quadratic lower bound forces divergence.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 152 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos152.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Erdős Problems 152 source record = https://www.erdosproblems.com/152
-- L4DEL: source: Paul Erdős and Pál Turán (1941), original paper, p. 212 = https://combinatorica.hu/~p_erdos/1941-01.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 152 = The proof establishes, for every Sidon set A of size n: 16 · I(A + A) + 100n + 16 ≥ n².
-- L4DEL: quote: Paul Erdős and Pál Turán (1941), original paper, p. 212 = suppose that the sums aᵢ+aⱼ (where i≤j) are all different. Such sequences, called B₂ sequences by Sidon

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_26.variants.tenenbaum.lean
@decl Erdos26.target_theorem_0
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős problem 26 variant: a thick sequence whose shifted multiples stay sparse
-- L4DEL: intuition: The universal weak-Behrend assertion is false. There is a strictly increasing sequence with divergent reciprocal sum such that every additive shift still leaves a fixed positive proportion of integers uncovered by its multiples.
-- L4DEL: step: Construct the sequence in finite arithmetic-progression blocks, with prime congruences assigned to each stage.
-- L4DEL: step: The reciprocal mass of the blocks diverges, so the resulting sequence is thick.
-- L4DEL: step: For any fixed shift, the congruence structure bounds the upper density of its multiples below `1-ε`, contradicting the claimed weak-Behrend conclusion.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 26 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos26.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Erdős Problems 26 source record = https://www.erdosproblems.com/26
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 26 = We construct the sequence iteratively in finite blocks

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_741.parts.i.lean
@decl Erdos741.S_x
-- L4DEL: title: The lower observation scale in the Erdős 741 counterexample
-- L4DEL: intuition: `S_x(k)=4^(3^k)` is the rapidly growing left endpoint of the `k`th scale. Base four makes the digit split carry-free, while the double-exponential spacing prevents earlier added intervals from controlling the next density measurement.
-- L4DEL: step: Raise 4 to `3^k`; the resulting scales race apart.
-- L4DEL: step: At `2*S_x(k)`, the proof measures how sparse the two monochromatic self-sumsets still are.
-- L4DEL: step: This Lean artifact uses the fixed-factor companion scale `S_y(k)=10*S_x(k)`; Ulam AI's written proof uses longer `k*N_k` intervals and explicitly distinguishes the constructions.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = The key point is that B1 + B2 = N0 , while the long intervals Ik force large local oscillations

@decl Erdos741.S_y
-- L4DEL: title: The upper endpoint: ten times the lower scale
-- L4DEL: intuition: `S_y(k)=10*S_x(k)` gives each added interval a fixed nine-scale-unit width. Those fat intervals later force many same-colour sums before the next enormous base-four scale arrives.
-- L4DEL: step: Start at `S_x(k)` and end at ten times that value.
-- L4DEL: step: Compare partial densities at `2*S_x(k)` and `2*S_y(k)`.
-- L4DEL: step: Do not identify this fixed-multiple construction with Ulam AI's fully written variant, whose interval length grows like `k*N_k`; the note says they share a mechanism, not identical intervals.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = Our proof uses the same underlying mechanism, but chooses the intervals Ik with length kNk rather than a fixed multiple of Nk .

@decl Erdos741.S_C
-- L4DEL: title: The sparse union of fat intervals that drives density oscillation
-- L4DEL: intuition: `S_C` is the union of every half-open interval `[S_x(k),S_y(k))`. Each interval is locally large, but the starting scales grow so quickly that earlier intervals become negligible at the next lower checkpoint.
-- L4DEL: step: Insert the full interval from `S_x(k)` through `S_y(k)-1` for every `k`.
-- L4DEL: step: The intervals later provide a large block in whichever colour receives their elements.
-- L4DEL: step: Their separation lets the proof alternate between sparse lower-scale behavior and dense upper-scale behavior.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = The key point is that B1 + B2 = N0 , while the long intervals Ik force large local oscillations

@decl Erdos741.split1
-- L4DEL: title: Put each binary digit into the 0-or-1 part of a base-four number
-- L4DEL: intuition: `split1(n)` recursively keeps the parity bit of each base-four digit. Its base-four digits are therefore only 0 or 1, which prevents carries when two `B1` elements are added.
-- L4DEL: step: Take `n % 2` as the current low digit.
-- L4DEL: step: Recurse on `n/4`, shifting the result left by one base-four place.
-- L4DEL: step: Paired with `split2`, the two digit streams add back to the original natural number.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = Every digit d ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3} has a unique decomposition

@decl Erdos741.split2
-- L4DEL: title: Put the complementary 0-or-2 contribution into the second base-four stream
-- L4DEL: intuition: `split2(n)` keeps `n % 4 - n % 2` at each digit, so its base-four digits are 0 or 2. Digit by digit, `split1(n)+split2(n)=n` without a carry.
-- L4DEL: step: Remove the parity contribution from the current base-four digit.
-- L4DEL: step: Recurse on `n/4` and shift by a factor of four.
-- L4DEL: step: The complementary digits are the coverage half of the construction; each half's self-sums remain sparse.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = Every digit d ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3} has a unique decomposition

@decl Erdos741.B1
-- L4DEL: title: The carry-free base-four set with digits 0 and 1
-- L4DEL: intuition: `B1` is the range of `split1`. Its elements use only 0-or-1 base-four digits, making `B1+B1` digitwise sparse even though `B1+B2` will cover every natural number.
-- L4DEL: step: Collect every output of `split1`.
-- L4DEL: step: Two such outputs can only produce base-four sum digits 0, 1, or 2.
-- L4DEL: step: This supplies one sparse monochromatic channel in the partition argument.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = The key point is that B1 + B2 = N0 , while the long intervals Ik force large local oscillations

@decl Erdos741.B2
-- L4DEL: title: The complementary carry-free base-four set with digits 0 and 2
-- L4DEL: intuition: `B2` is the range of `split2`. It is the digitwise complement to `B1`: together the two sets represent every natural, while `B2+B2` has the same sparse-count phenomenon as `B1+B1`.
-- L4DEL: step: Collect every output of `split2`.
-- L4DEL: step: Its digits are 0 or 2, equivalently twice the 0-or-1 digit pattern.
-- L4DEL: step: Pair each `split2(n)` with `split1(n)` to recover `n`.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = The key point is that B1 + B2 = N0 , while the long intervals Ik force large local oscillations

@decl Erdos741.SandorA
-- L4DEL: title: The strict-natural-density counterexample set
-- L4DEL: intuition: `SandorA` joins the carry-free covering set `B1∪B2` to the sparse union of fat intervals `S_C`. Coverage makes `SandorA+SandorA` all naturals; the intervals make every two-colouring oscillate too much for both monochromatic self-sumsets to have positive natural-density limits.
-- L4DEL: step: Begin with `B1∪B2`, whose cross-sums cover all naturals.
-- L4DEL: step: Add the fixed-factor intervals `[S_x(k),S_y(k))`, with `S_y(k)=10*S_x(k)`, to force large local blocks of same-colour sums.
-- L4DEL: step: Use widely separated checkpoints to turn these two features into a convergence contradiction.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = Our proof uses the same underlying mechanism, but chooses the intervals Ik with length kNk rather than a fixed multiple of Nk .

@decl Erdos741.B1_sum_ncard
-- L4DEL: title: Below `4^d`, the first self-sumset has at most `3^d` values
-- L4DEL: intuition: A sum of two 0-or-1 base-four digit strings never has digit 3. There are only three choices at each of the first `d` positions, hence at most `3^d` possible sums below `4^d`.
-- L4DEL: step: Prove each base-four digit of a `B1+B1` value lies in `{0,1,2}`.
-- L4DEL: step: Encode those `d` digits as a base-three number.
-- L4DEL: step: Injectivity gives the cardinality bound `3^d`, negligible beside `4^d`.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = Every digit d ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3} has a unique decomposition

@decl Erdos741.B2_sum_ncard
-- L4DEL: title: The second carry-free self-sumset obeys the same `3^d` bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: Because the 0-or-2 digit set is twice the 0-or-1 digit set, its self-sums below the matching power-of-four scale also have at most `3^d` possibilities.
-- L4DEL: step: Reduce `B2` digits to the corresponding `B1` digit pattern.
-- L4DEL: step: Transfer the no-fourth-digit-choice counting argument.
-- L4DEL: step: This keeps both pure self-sum channels sparse at the lower checkpoints.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = Every digit d ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3} has a unique decomposition

@decl Erdos741.B1_add_B2_eq_univ
-- L4DEL: title: The two complementary digit sets add to every natural number
-- L4DEL: intuition: For each natural `n`, the construction splits every base-four digit uniquely between `split1(n)` and `split2(n)`. Thus `n` belongs to `B1+B2`, and the cross-sumset is exactly all naturals.
-- L4DEL: step: Choose `split1(n)` from `B1` and `split2(n)` from `B2`.
-- L4DEL: step: Apply the digitwise identity `split1(n)+split2(n)=n`.
-- L4DEL: step: Since `n` was arbitrary, conclude `B1+B2=Set.univ`.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = Every digit d ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3} has a unique decomposition

@decl Erdos741.SandorA_add_SandorA_eq_univ
-- L4DEL: title: The counterexample's full self-sumset is all naturals
-- L4DEL: intuition: `SandorA` contains both `B1` and `B2`, and their cross-sumset already equals every natural number. Enlarging the summand set cannot lose those representations, so `SandorA+SandorA=Set.univ`.
-- L4DEL: step: Embed `B1+B2` into `(B1∪B2)+(B1∪B2)`.
-- L4DEL: step: Embed that sumset into `SandorA+SandorA`.
-- L4DEL: step: Use `B1+B2=Set.univ`; a superset of the universe is the universe.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = The key point is that B1 + B2 = N0 , while the long intervals Ik force large local oscillations

@decl Erdos741.SandorA_has_pos_density
-- L4DEL: title: The full sumset has natural density one
-- L4DEL: intuition: Since `SandorA+SandorA` is `Set.univ`, its partial-density sequence is constantly one after the zero scale. This proves `HasPosDensity` in the strict sense used here: a positive natural-density limit exists.
-- L4DEL: step: Rewrite the self-sumset as `Set.univ`.
-- L4DEL: step: Use the theorem that the universe has density 1.
-- L4DEL: step: Package that limit with `1>0` as `HasPosDensity`.
-- L4DEL: source: Formal Conjectures density definitions = https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/blob/1646ca16afd6cc7a693d3bdc9f066c4d3cc01a89/FormalConjecturesForMathlib/Data/Set/Density.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = The key point is that B1 + B2 = N0 , while the long intervals Ik force large local oscillations

@decl Erdos741.B_partition_alpha_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Any convergent two-colour split pays a cross-term density deficit
-- L4DEL: intuition: If the two monochromatic self-sumsets have natural-density limits `α₁` and `α₂`, then the carry-free digit split forces `α₁+α₂ ≤ 1-α₁α₂`. In particular, if both limits are positive, their sum is uniformly below one.
-- L4DEL: step: Inspect each colour's proportions inside the `B1` and `B2` digit sets at the lower scales.
-- L4DEL: step: Bound pure-colour self-sums by the matching products of those proportions; mixed digit sums occupy the missing cross term.
-- L4DEL: step: Pass to the assumed limits and remove an arbitrary epsilon to obtain the exact inequality.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = Taking k → ∞ yields

@decl Erdos741.sandor_cross_sums
-- L4DEL: title: A fat interval forces almost twice its size in monochromatic sums
-- L4DEL: intuition: Partition the interval `[S_x(k),S_y(k))` between two colours. For each colour, a finite nonempty set `U` satisfies `|U+U|≥2|U|-1`; adding the two bounds yields at least `2*(S_y-S_x)-2` same-colour sums in the doubled interval.
-- L4DEL: step: Intersect each colour with the whole fat interval; the two intersections partition it.
-- L4DEL: step: Apply the elementary sumset lower bound separately to both pieces.
-- L4DEL: step: Add the bounds and place all resulting sums between `2*S_x(k)` and `2*S_y(k)`.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = Propositions 3.3 and 3.4 would give the contradiction

@decl Erdos741.SandorA_fluctuation_bounds
-- L4DEL: title: Positive limiting densities would still oscillate by a fixed amount
-- L4DEL: intuition: Assuming both monochromatic self-sumsets have positive natural-density limits, this lemma constructs lower checkpoints `N(k)=2*S_x(k)` and upper checkpoints `M(k)=2*S_y(k)` where their combined partial density at `M(k)` exceeds that at `N(k)` by one fixed `delta>0` forever.
-- L4DEL: step: Positivity and the partition bound give a fixed deficit below one at the lower checkpoints.
-- L4DEL: step: The fat interval contributes enough new same-colour sums to recover more than that deficit at the upper checkpoints.
-- L4DEL: step: Both checkpoint sequences tend to infinity, so the permanent gap is incompatible with convergence to `α₁+α₂`.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = Propositions 3.3 and 3.4 would give the contradiction

@decl Erdos741.SandorA_no_valid_partition
-- L4DEL: title: No disjoint two-part split can give both self-sumsets positive natural density
-- L4DEL: intuition: Suppose both component self-sumsets had positive limits. Convergence would make each combined partial density arbitrarily close to `α₁+α₂` at every sufficiently large scale, but `SandorA_fluctuation_bounds` produces two arbitrarily large scales separated by a fixed positive gap.
-- L4DEL: step: Unpack the two `HasPosDensity` hypotheses into positive limits and convergence proofs.
-- L4DEL: step: Choose upper and lower convergence bounds with tolerance `delta/4` for both colours.
-- L4DEL: step: Evaluate at a sufficiently large fluctuation checkpoint and derive the numerical contradiction.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = Propositions 3.3 and 3.4 would give the contradiction

@decl Erdos741.exists_erdos_counterexample
-- L4DEL: title: The explicit witness refuting the strict-natural-density statement
-- L4DEL: intuition: `SandorA` has a self-sumset of natural density one, yet every disjoint two-part split fails to give positive natural-density limits to both component self-sumsets. This is the witness needed to negate the universal strict-density version of Erdős 741(i).
-- L4DEL: step: Use `SandorA` as the existential set.
-- L4DEL: step: Supply `SandorA_has_pos_density` for its full self-sumset.
-- L4DEL: step: Supply `SandorA_no_valid_partition` for every proposed partition.
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = For ordinary natural density, the first question has a negative answer. For upper density, the first question has a positive answer.

@decl Erdos741.target_theorem_0
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős problem 741(i): strict natural density is false, upper density is true
-- L4DEL: intuition: This Lean theorem proves `answer(False)` for `HasPosDensity`, which here means that a positive ordinary natural-density limit exists. The pinned companion proves a different, compatible theorem for positive upper density. The two artifacts do not disagree: density choice changes the answer. The lower-density variant is separate and remains unresolved in the cited status sources.
-- L4DEL: step: The Lean development builds `SandorA`, whose full self-sumset is all naturals and therefore has natural density one.
-- L4DEL: step: Its carry-free digit sets make monochromatic self-sums sparse at lower checkpoints, while added fixed-factor fat intervals force many such sums at upper checkpoints.
-- L4DEL: step: Any partition with two positive natural-density limits would therefore oscillate by a fixed amount, contradicting convergence; this supplies the negative witness.
-- L4DEL: step: The companion's alternating-block argument concerns upper density, where only a positive limsup is required, so it supports the separate affirmative variant and does not explain this Lean proof.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 741(i) = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos741i.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned AlphaProof Lean result for Erdős 741(i) = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_741.parts.i.lean#L1486-L1503
-- L4DEL: source: Formal Conjectures density definitions = https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/blob/1646ca16afd6cc7a693d3bdc9f066c4d3cc01a89/FormalConjecturesForMathlib/Data/Set/Density.lean
-- L4DEL: source: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = https://www.ulam.ai/research/erdos741.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Paul Erdős, Some of my favourite problems which recently have been solved (1994), p. 263 = https://mathematica-pannonica.ttk.pte.hu/articles/mp05-2/mp05-2-261-269.pdf#page=3
-- L4DEL: source: Erdős Problems 741 current status record = https://www.erdosproblems.com/741
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 741(i) = We show that the answer is yes. We use an alternating block partition.
-- L4DEL: quote: Ulam AI, A Note on Erdős Problem #741 (2026) = For ordinary natural density, the first question has a negative answer. For upper density, the first question has a positive answer.
-- L4DEL: quote: Paul Erdős, Some of my favourite problems which recently have been solved (1994), p. 263 = Can one always decompose the sequence A as the union of two disjoint subsequences

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_741.parts.ii.lean
@decl Erdos741.target_theorem_0
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős problem 741(ii): an order-two basis with no syndetic two-part split
-- L4DEL: intuition: There exists an additive basis `A ∪ {0}` of order two such that every disjoint partition `A=A₁∪A₂` leaves at least one of `A₁+A₁` and `A₂+A₂` with arbitrarily large gaps.
-- L4DEL: step: Build `A` from dense clumps separated by rapidly growing forbidden zones, retaining one specially placed oasis in each zone.
-- L4DEL: step: Every natural number is still a sum of two elements of `A ∪ {0}`, either directly, by halving, or by using the oasis.
-- L4DEL: step: In any two-colouring of `A`, the oasis has one colour; a long interval of sums then becomes unavailable to the other colour, destroying syndeticity.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 741(ii) = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos741ii.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Erdős Problems 741 source record = https://www.erdosproblems.com/741
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 741(ii) = We show that the answer is yes by constructing a pathological basis A with “forbidden zones”

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_846.lean
@decl Erdos846.target_theorem_0
-- L4DEL: title: Erdős problem 846: robustly non-collinear sets need not split into finitely many general-position sets
-- L4DEL: intuition: The asserted implication is false. An infinite planar set can have the property that every finite subset contains a line-free subset of at least half its size, yet the whole set is not a finite union of sets with no three collinear.
-- L4DEL: step: Map each edge of the countably infinite complete graph to a planar point using a rapidly growing algebraic parametrization.
-- L4DEL: step: The construction makes three image points collinear exactly when their three edges form a triangle.
-- L4DEL: step: Triangle-free subgraphs supply large non-collinear subsets, while finite colourings of the complete graph force a monochromatic triangle and rule out any finite decomposition.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 846 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/ErdosProblems/erdos846.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Erdős Problems 846 source record = https://www.erdosproblems.com/846
-- L4DEL: source: Moe Putterman, Mehtaab Sawhney, and Gregory Valiant (2026), arXiv v1 abstract = https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21275v1
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for Erdős 846 = Three points in A are collinear if and only if their corresponding edges form a triangle in K∞.
-- L4DEL: quote: Moe Putterman, Mehtaab Sawhney, and Gregory Valiant (2026), arXiv v1 abstract = A cannot be separated into finitely many subsets such that each subset has no three points collinear

@file APNOutputs/OEIS/oeis_51293_conjecture_0.lean
@decl target_theorem_0
-- L4DEL: title: OEIS A051293: a six-term asymptotic expansion for subsets with integer average
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let `a(n)` count the nonempty subsets of `{1,…,n}` whose arithmetic mean is an integer. After subtracting the displayed expansion through `541/n⁵`, the error is little-o of `2^(n+1)/n⁶`; the Lean limit states that normalized remainder tends to zero.
-- L4DEL: step: Replace “integer average” by divisibility of the subset sum by its cardinality.
-- L4DEL: step: A roots-of-unity filter counts the size-`k` subsets satisfying that divisibility condition and separates the principal term from oscillatory terms.
-- L4DEL: step: Summing and estimating those terms produces the coefficients `1, 1, 3, 13, 75, 541` and controls the normalized remainder.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written proof companion for OEIS A051293 = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/OEIS/oeis_51293.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Benoit Cloitre's OEIS A051293 comment (2002) = https://oeis.org/A051293
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written proof companion for OEIS A051293 = has an integer average if and only if the sum of its elements is divisible by its cardinality |S|.
-- L4DEL: quote: Benoit Cloitre's OEIS A051293 comment (2002) = a(n) is asymptotic to 2^(n+1)/n.

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/AdditiveCombinatorics/57.lean
@decl Green57.green_57
-- L4DEL: title: Ben Green's Problem 57: the two structured-function spaces do not always coincide
-- L4DEL: intuition: The proposed equality `Φ(G) = Φ'(G)` is false for finite abelian groups. The pinned development constructs a separating real linear functional for `G = ZMod 3`, so this single finite counterexample refutes the universal equality.
-- L4DEL: step: `Φ(G)` is the real convex hull of bounded three-kernel convolutions; `Φ'(G)` restricts the third kernel to depend only on `x₁+x₂`.
-- L4DEL: step: For `ZMod 3`, the auxiliary theorem uses the coefficient vector `(-1,-3,3)` and proves that its support function is strictly larger on `Φ` than on `Φ'`.
-- L4DEL: step: Equal sets have equal support functions, so the strict separation proves `Φ(ZMod 3) ≠ Φ'(ZMod 3)` and therefore negates the quantified claim.
-- L4DEL: step: The original problem says "abelian group"; this Lean theorem quantifies over finite abelian groups with decidable equality, which is enough because the counterexample is finite.
-- L4DEL: source: Ben Green, 100 Open Problems, Problem 57 = https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/greenbj/papers/open-problems.pdf#page=28
-- L4DEL: source: George Tsoukalas et al., AlphaProof Nexus paper (Section 4) = https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22763v2
-- L4DEL: quote: Ben Green, 100 Open Problems, Problem 57 = One would guess that the answer is probably ‘no’.
-- L4DEL: quote: George Tsoukalas et al., AlphaProof Nexus paper (Section 4) = Our agent helped resolve problem #57 from Green’s well-known list of open conjectures

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Optimization/LastIterateConvergence.lean
@decl Arxiv.«1905.10899».last_iterate_convergence
-- L4DEL: title: Exact last-iterate `O(1/T)` convergence for anchored gradient descent-ascent
-- L4DEL: intuition: For a differentiable convex-concave objective whose saddle operator is monotone and Lipschitz, the schedule `α_t = 1/(K√(t+γ))` and `β_t = γ/(t+γ)` with `γ ≥ 2` gives a positive constant `C` such that `‖F(z_T)‖² ≤ C/T` for every `T ≥ 1`.
-- L4DEL: step: The hypotheses explicitly supply differentiability, monotonicity, a positive Lipschitz constant, and a saddle point `z_star` satisfying `F(z_star)=0`.
-- L4DEL: step: The sequence is not arbitrary: each state must follow the pinned update rule with the square-root step size and reciprocal anchoring schedule.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof first bounds every iterate relative to the saddle point, then bounds consecutive-iterate differences, and finally rewrites the update to control the last operator value.
-- L4DEL: step: The conclusion is existential in `C`; the proof constructs one, but the public theorem promises only that some positive uniform constant works.
-- L4DEL: source: Anja Surina et al., improved anchored-GDA paper (2026) = https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.03782v1
-- L4DEL: source: Ernest K. Ryu, Kun Yuan, and Wotao Yin, original anchored-SimGD paper (2020 revision) = https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.10899v3
-- L4DEL: source: George Tsoukalas et al., AlphaProof Nexus paper (Section 4) = https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22763v2
-- L4DEL: quote: Anja Surina et al., improved anchored-GDA paper (2026) = it remained an open problem whether the improved exact O(1/t) rate is achievable
-- L4DEL: quote: Ernest K. Ryu, Kun Yuan, and Wotao Yin, original anchored-SimGD paper (2020 revision) = If p ∈ (1/2, 1) and γ ≥ 2, then SimGD-A converges
-- L4DEL: quote: George Tsoukalas et al., AlphaProof Nexus paper (Section 4) = The proof departs from continuous-time ordinary differential equations (ODE) analysis used in previous work, instead using a discrete-time recurrence-based approach.

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/Graphs/GraphConjecture2.lean
@decl WrittenOnTheWallII.GraphConjecture2.conjecture2
-- L4DEL: title: Graffiti.pc Conjecture 2: average local independence forces many spanning-tree leaves
-- L4DEL: intuition: In every finite connected simple graph, the maximum number `Ls(G)` of leaves in a spanning tree is at least `2(l(G)-1)`, where `l(G)` is the average, over vertices, of the largest independent set inside each open neighbourhood.
-- L4DEL: step: Choose a maximum independent set in every vertex neighbourhood and let `c(x)` count how many of those chosen sets contain `x`; double counting identifies the average of `c` with `l(G)`.
-- L4DEL: step: For every edge `xy`, independence and a spanning-tree construction bound `c(x)+c(y)` by `Ls(G)+2`.
-- L4DEL: step: Vertices with `c(x) > Ls(G)/2+1` form an independent heavy set, and each light vertex has only boundedly many heavy neighbours.
-- L4DEL: step: A discharging inequality moves each heavy vertex's excess to light neighbours without exceeding their slack, proving `l(G) ≤ Ls(G)/2+1` and hence the claimed result.
-- L4DEL: source: George Tsoukalas et al., AlphaProof Nexus paper (Appendix I) = https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22763v2
-- L4DEL: source: Independent proof by Yanmohan Wang, Tianyue Dai, and Rui Tong (2026) = https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.24020v1
-- L4DEL: quote: George Tsoukalas et al., AlphaProof Nexus paper (Appendix I) = The key idea is to look at the degrees of the vertices on the right side
-- L4DEL: quote: Independent proof by Yanmohan Wang, Tianyue Dai, and Rui Tong (2026) = We prove Graffiti.pc Conjecture 2, a 1996 conjecture listed as open

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/QuantumOptics/35.lean
@decl OpenQuantumProblem35.ame_11_5_open
-- L4DEL: title: An explicit absolutely maximally entangled state for 11 five-level parties
-- L4DEL: intuition: There exists a normalized pure state of 11 parties, each with local dimension 5, whose reduced density matrix on every choice of 5 parties is maximally mixed. The theorem is an existence result with a fully explicit graph-state witness; it does not claim that this is the first mathematical construction of the underlying quantum code.
-- L4DEL: step: `explicit_A` is an 11-by-11 symmetric, zero-diagonal circulant matrix over `ZMod 5`, built from the row `[0,0,0,1,2,2,2,2,1,0,0]`.
-- L4DEL: step: The finite `explicit_A_rank` computation proves that, after every permutation of the parties, distinct configurations on the first five parties are separated by at least one of the remaining six columns.
-- L4DEL: step: That rank condition makes the off-diagonal entries of every five-party reduced density matrix vanish; normalization and the diagonal calculation identify the whole reduction with the maximally mixed matrix.
-- L4DEL: step: `yes_AME_11_5` supplies `graphState explicit_A` as the witness, and `ame_11_5_open` packages the result as `answer(True) ↔ ExistsAME 11 5` without using `sorry` in this declaration.
-- L4DEL: step: Priority boundary: Mario Krenn's public account says AlphaProof Nexus found this AME state but also records that the underlying quantum code had already been discovered two years earlier. This annotation therefore credits the checked construction without calling it a first discovery.
-- L4DEL: source: Wolfram Helwig et al., Absolute Maximal Entanglement and Quantum Secret Sharing (2012) = https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.2289
-- L4DEL: source: Open Quantum Problems, Problem 35 = https://oqp.iqoqi.oeaw.ac.at/existence-of-absolutely-maximally-entangled-pure-states
-- L4DEL: source: Mario Krenn's account of the Nexus quantum-optics results and priority correction = https://www.linkedin.com/posts/mario-krenn-813b9512b_super-cool-paper-by-google-deepmind-on-ai4math-activity-7464334199462748160-6EG6
-- L4DEL: quote: Wolfram Helwig et al., Absolute Maximal Entanglement and Quantum Secret Sharing (2012) = AME states are characterized by being maximally entangled for all bipartitions of the system
-- L4DEL: quote: Mario Krenn's account of the Nexus quantum-optics results and priority correction = AlphaProof Nexus indeed did find a new AME state AME(11,5). However, it turned out that the underlying Quantum Code was already discovered 2 years ago

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/QuantumOptics/MonochromaticQuantumGraph.lean
@decl MonochromaticQuantumGraph.eqSystem_no_solution_even_ge4_d_eq_n_explicit
-- L4DEL: title: No equal-dimension monochromatic quantum graph for even numbers of parties at least four
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every even `N ≥ 4`, no complex edge-weight family on the complete `N`-vertex graph with `N` endpoint colors satisfies the monochromatic quantum-graph equations: every constant vertex coloring should have perfect-matching amplitude 1, while every nonconstant coloring should have amplitude 0. This is the equal-dimension case `D = N`, not the still broader conjecture for every `D ≥ 3` when `N ≥ 6`.
-- L4DEL: step: `EqSystemN N N W` is the exact algebraic interface: it sums products of the selected edge weights over all perfect matchings for each assignment of colors to vertices.
-- L4DEL: step: In the separately linked proof-bearing Formal Conjectures artifact, the argument constructs product test vectors whose contractions annihilate every contribution incident to a distinguished vertex, forcing the graph-side evaluation to be zero.
-- L4DEL: step: The same test vectors give a nonzero evaluation of the desired monochromatic right-hand side. Equality of the two evaluations would therefore make a nonzero complex number equal to zero, a contradiction.
-- L4DEL: step: Trust boundary: this AlphaProof Nexus results wrapper ends in `by sorry`, so this corpus page must retain a local `sorry` finding. The linked Formal Conjectures source is a separate artifact and is not silently substituted into this build.
-- L4DEL: source: George Tsoukalas et al., AlphaProof Nexus paper (Section 4) = https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22763v2
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned proof-bearing Formal Conjectures artifact = https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/blob/af88acbf9da0f26e3e934743a819e986e02f6875/FormalConjectures/Paper/MonochromaticQuantumGraph.lean#L1006-L1020
-- L4DEL: source: Mario Krenn, Xuemei Gu, and Anton Zeilinger, Quantum Experiments and Graphs (2017) = https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.06646v2
-- L4DEL: source: Mario Krenn's account of the Nexus quantum-optics results = https://www.linkedin.com/posts/mario-krenn-813b9512b_super-cool-paper-by-google-deepmind-on-ai4math-activity-7464334199462748160-6EG6
-- L4DEL: quote: George Tsoukalas et al., AlphaProof Nexus paper (Section 4) = Our agent resolved multiple conjectures of this form, in particular with N = d ∈ {4, 6, 10}.
-- L4DEL: quote: Mario Krenn, Xuemei Gu, and Anton Zeilinger, Quantum Experiments and Graphs (2017) = Every term in the emerging quantum superposition corresponds to a perfect matching in the graph.
-- L4DEL: quote: Mario Krenn's account of the Nexus quantum-optics results = one cannot generate monochromatic quantum graphs with dimensions d equal or larger than the number of vertices of the graph

@file APNOutputs/AICollaborator/AlgebraicGeometry/hilbert_functions_1.lean
@decl Monomial
-- L4DEL: title: A monomial as its vector of natural-number exponents
-- L4DEL: intuition: The Lean type `Monomial(r)` is the finitely supported exponent vector for a monomial in `r` variables. For the manuscript's three-variable theorem, a monomial such as `x₀^a x₁^b x₂^c` is therefore represented by the vector `(a,b,c)` in `ℕ³`.
-- L4DEL: step: A coordinate records the exponent of one variable, so comparing two values pointwise is the same divisibility order used throughout the proof.
-- L4DEL: step: The Lean definition works for any finite number `r` of variables; the companion specializes this representation to `r = 3`.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/hilbert.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = the exponent vector m = (m(0), m(1), m(2)) ∈ N3

@decl totalDeg
-- L4DEL: title: Total degree is the sum of a monomial's exponents
-- L4DEL: intuition: `totalDeg(m)`—written `totalDeg m` in Lean—adds every coordinate of the exponent vector. In three variables this is `m(0)+m(1)+m(2)`, the usual total degree of the represented monomial.
-- L4DEL: step: The finitely supported sum makes the definition uniform in the number of variables.
-- L4DEL: step: Degree is the grading used both to group the order ideal into its O-sequence and to state that all maximal monomials of a pure ideal lie in one degree.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/hilbert.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = |m| = m(0) + m(1) + m(2) for total degree.

@decl OrderIdeal
-- L4DEL: title: A finite set of monomials closed downward under divisibility
-- L4DEL: intuition: An `OrderIdeal(r)` (Lean: `OrderIdeal r`) stores a finite collection of monomials together with the guarantee that every divisor of a stored monomial is stored too. In exponent-vector form, divisibility is coordinatewise `≤`.
-- L4DEL: step: `carrier` is the finite set itself.
-- L4DEL: step: `downward_closed` is the proof that if `n` is in the carrier and `m ≤ n`, then `m` is also in it.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/hilbert.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Richard P. Stanley, Hilbert functions of graded algebras (1978) = https://math.mit.edu/~rstan/pubs/pubfiles/30.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = A finite order ideal Γ ⊂ N3 is a finite set closed downward under this order
-- L4DEL: quote: Richard P. Stanley, Hilbert functions of graded algebras (1978) = is said to be an O-sequence if there exists an order ideal M of monomials

@decl divisorsFinset
-- L4DEL: title: All monomial divisors of one generator as a finite set
-- L4DEL: intuition: `divisorsFinset(g)` (Lean: `divisorsFinset g`) is the principal lower set of `g`: it contains exactly the monomials `u` whose exponent vector is coordinatewise at most that of `g`. Equivalently, these are the monomial divisors of `g`.
-- L4DEL: step: In the type-two proof, every element of the pure order ideal lies below one of its two maximal monomials.
-- L4DEL: step: This lets the carrier be rewritten as the union of two such divisor sets, the first combinatorial reduction in the manuscript proof.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/hilbert.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = Div(g) = {u : u ≤ g}.

@decl IsMaximalIn
-- L4DEL: title: Maximality inside an order ideal under monomial divisibility
-- L4DEL: intuition: `IsMaximalIn(I,m)` (Lean: `IsMaximalIn I m`) says first that `m` belongs to `I`, and then that no strictly larger monomial of `I` is divisible by `m`. Any member `n` above `m` must also lie below `m`, hence equals it in the partial order.
-- L4DEL: step: The condition is relative to the selected order ideal, not maximality among all monomials.
-- L4DEL: step: These maximal elements are the generators whose shared degree and number determine purity, socle degree, and type.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/hilbert.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = A monomial m ∈ Γ is maximal if every n ∈ Γ with m ≤ n also satisfies n ≤ m.

@decl IsPure
-- L4DEL: title: Purity means every maximal monomial has the same total degree
-- L4DEL: intuition: `IsPure(I)` (Lean: `IsPure I`) requires any two maximal monomials of `I` to have equal total degree. It does not itself assert that a maximal monomial exists; that nonemptiness is added by `PureOrderIdeal`.
-- L4DEL: step: The equal-degree condition supplies one common top grading for the ideal.
-- L4DEL: step: That common degree becomes the socle degree used as the endpoint of the finite O-sequence.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/hilbert.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = The ideal is pure if all maximal monomials have the same total degree

@decl PureOrderIdeal
-- L4DEL: title: An order ideal packaged with purity and a maximal monomial
-- L4DEL: intuition: `PureOrderIdeal(r)` (Lean: `PureOrderIdeal r`) is the structure used when later statements need a pure finite order ideal as data. It contains the ideal, a proof that its maximal monomials share one degree, and a proof that the maximal set is nonempty.
-- L4DEL: step: The nonempty field makes the subsequent choice of a maximal monomial—and therefore the definition of socle degree—total rather than conditional.
-- L4DEL: step: This is a formal packaging choice around the manuscript's mathematical notion of a pure order ideal, not a stronger log-concavity assertion.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/hilbert.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = The ideal is pure if all maximal monomials have the same total degree

@decl typePO
-- L4DEL: title: The type is the number of maximal monomials
-- L4DEL: intuition: `typePO(P)` is the cardinality of `maximalMonomials(P.ideal)`. In Lean these are written `typePO P` and `maximalMonomials P.ideal`. Thus “type 2” in the theorem means that the pure order ideal has exactly two maximal monomial generators.
-- L4DEL: step: The definition counts maximal monomials; it does not count variables, degrees, or all elements of the ideal.
-- L4DEL: step: The main proof uses type two to name two distinct maxima and express the carrier as the union of their divisor sets.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/hilbert.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = The type is the number of maximal monomials.

@decl socleDegree
-- L4DEL: title: The shared total degree of a pure ideal's maximal monomials
-- L4DEL: intuition: `socleDegree(P)` (Lean: `socleDegree P`) chooses one maximal monomial and returns its total degree. Purity proves that every other maximal monomial has the same degree, so the result is independent of which witness is chosen.
-- L4DEL: step: Nonemptiness of the maximal set supplies the chosen witness.
-- L4DEL: step: The socle degree is the top index through which unimodality or log-concavity is asserted.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/hilbert.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = this common degree is the socle degree.

@decl pureOSequence
-- L4DEL: title: Degree-by-degree counts of monomials in an order ideal
-- L4DEL: intuition: `pureOSequence(I,d)` (Lean: `pureOSequence I d`) counts the monomials in `I` whose total degree is exactly `d`. Varying `d` produces the Hilbert-function sequence studied by the theorem.
-- L4DEL: step: The carrier is filtered by the equation `totalDeg m = d`.
-- L4DEL: step: The cardinality of that filtered finite set is the value of the sequence at degree `d`.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/hilbert.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Richard P. Stanley, Hilbert functions of graded algebras (1978) = https://math.mit.edu/~rstan/pubs/pubfiles/30.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = The pure O-sequence of Γ is hΓ (d) = #{m ∈ Γ : |m| = d}.
-- L4DEL: quote: Richard P. Stanley, Hilbert functions of graded algebras (1978) = is said to be an O-sequence if there exists an order ideal M of monomials

@decl IsLogConcaveUpTo
-- L4DEL: title: Log-concavity at every internal degree through a chosen endpoint
-- L4DEL: intuition: `IsLogConcaveUpTo(e,h)` (Lean: `IsLogConcaveUpTo e h`) requires `h(i-1)·h(i+1) ≤ h(i)²` for every integer index strictly between `0` and `e`. Each middle term must therefore dominate the geometric mean of its two neighbours.
-- L4DEL: step: `Finset.Ioo 0 e` makes both boundary exclusions explicit: neither `0` nor `e` is tested.
-- L4DEL: step: For the pure O-sequence theorem, `e` is instantiated with the socle degree.
-- L4DEL: source: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results/blob/0647711a71183c1ea492ad60860776617ce1ea88/NaturalLanguageProofs/AICollaborator/hilbert.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Human-written Hilbert-functions proof companion = hΓ (i − 1)hΓ (i + 1) ≤ hΓ (i)2 (0 < i < e).

@file APNOutputs/ErdosProblems/erdos_12.parts.ii.lean
@decl Erdos12.IsGood
-- L4DEL: title: Property P: no member divides the sum of two larger distinct members
-- L4DEL: intuition: `IsGood A` means that `A` is infinite and whenever `a,b,c ∈ A`, `a ∣ b+c`, `a<b`, and `a<c`, the two larger terms must coincide. Equivalently, no three distinct members realize the forbidden divisibility pattern.
-- L4DEL: step: The conclusion `b = c` is how the Lean predicate permits repeated use of one larger element while excluding a triple of distinct elements.
-- L4DEL: step: This is the formal version of the property introduced by Paul Erdős and András Sárközy; their ordered-sequence wording and the set-based Lean wording express the same restriction.
-- L4DEL: source: Paul Erdős and András Sárközy (1970), p. 97 = https://renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1970-13.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: Paul Erdős and András Sárközy (1970), p. 97 = divides the sum of two larger terms

