@file APNOutputs/OEIS/a091669_conjecture_primitive_root.lean
@decl a
-- L4DEL: title: A091669: $a(n) = \frac{2^{n-1}}{n!} \prod_{k=1}^{n-1} (2^k-1)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: A091669: $a(n) = \frac{2^{n-1}}{n!} \prod_{k=1}^{n-1} (2^k-1)$. The sequence $a(n)$ is composed of natural numbers, thus we define it as a function $\mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$.

@file APNOutputs/OEIS/A224515_conjecture_existence.lean
@decl A224515
-- L4DEL: title: A224515: $a(n) = \text{least } k \text{ such that } \sqrt{k^2 \operatorname{XOR} (k+1)^2} = 2n+1, \text{ } a(n) = -1 \text{ if there is no such } k$
-- L4DEL: intuition: A224515: $a(n) = \text{least } k \text{ such that } \sqrt{k^2 \operatorname{XOR} (k+1)^2} = 2n+1, \text{ } a(n) = -1 \text{ if there is no such } k$. This is equivalent to finding the smallest $k \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $k^2 \oplus (k+1)^2 = (2n+1)^2$. We use the set infimum ($\operatorname{sInf}$) to denote the least element of the set of natural numbers satisfying the condition. Since Mathlib's `sInf` on a subset of `ℕ` gives a result in `ℕ`, this definition is only completely faithful to the OEIS when the set is non-empty. The OEIS definition implies that the set of k's is non-empty for all n.

@file APNOutputs/OEIS/A309132_conjecture_carmichael.lean
@decl a
-- L4DEL: title: A309132: a(n) is the denominator of F(n) = A027641(n-1)/n + A027642(n-1)/n^2
-- L4DEL: intuition: A309132: a(n) is the denominator of F(n) = A027641(n-1)/n + A027642(n-1)/n^2.

@file APNOutputs/OEIS/A309132_conjecture_carmichael.lean
@decl is_carmichael_number
-- L4DEL: title: Definition of a Carmichael number $n$: a composite number s.t
-- L4DEL: intuition: Definition of a Carmichael number $n$: a composite number s.t. $b^{n-1} \equiv 1 \pmod n$ for all $b$ coprime to $n$.

@file APNOutputs/OEIS/A309132_conjecture_carmichael.lean
@decl is_composite
-- L4DEL: title: Helper definition for "composite number"
-- L4DEL: intuition: Helper definition for "composite number"

@file APNOutputs/OEIS/a325046_odd_terms_at_k_times_k_plus_1.lean
@decl a
-- L4DEL: title: A325046: G.f.: $\sum_{n \ge 0} x^n \cdot \frac{(1 + x^n)^n}{(1 - x^{n+1})^{n+1}}$
-- L4DEL: intuition: A325046: G.f.: $\sum_{n \ge 0} x^n \cdot \frac{(1 + x^n)^n}{(1 - x^{n+1})^{n+1}}$. The term $a(N)$ is the coefficient of $x^N$ in the generating function. Expanding the terms, we get a formula for $a(N)$: $$a(N) = \sum_{n=0}^N \sum_{k=0}^n \mathbf{1}_{n + nk + (n+1)j = N} \binom{n}{k} \binom{n+j}{j}$$ where $j = \frac{N - n(k+1)}{n+1}$.

@file APNOutputs/OEIS/A382590_conjecture_kth_prime_factor_is_eventually_periodic.lean
@decl A382590
-- L4DEL: title: A382590
-- L4DEL: intuition: A382590: $a(n)$ is the sequence defined by the mutual recurrence relations: $a(n) = a(n-1)b(n-2) + a(n-2)b(n-1)$ and $b(n) = a(n-1)b(n-2) - a(n-2)b(n-1)$ starting with $a(0) = b(0) = b(1) = 1$ and a(1) = 2. The terms are in $\mathbb{Z}$ due to negative values.

@file APNOutputs/OEIS/A382590_conjecture_kth_prime_factor_is_eventually_periodic.lean
@decl A382590_pair
-- L4DEL: title: Helper function for A382590, computing the pair $(a(n), b(n))$ such that: $a(n) = a(n-1)b(n-2) + a(n-2)b(n-1)$ $b(n) = a(n-1)b(n-2) - a(n-2)b(n-1)$
-- L4DEL: intuition: Helper function for A382590, computing the pair $(a(n), b(n))$ such that: $a(n) = a(n-1)b(n-2) + a(n-2)b(n-1)$ $b(n) = a(n-1)b(n-2) - a(n-2)b(n-1)$

@file APNOutputs/OEIS/A382590_conjecture_kth_prime_factor_is_eventually_periodic.lean
@decl kth_prime_factor
-- L4DEL: title: The k-th prime factor of an integer n (where k>=1), counted with multiplicity
-- L4DEL: intuition: The k-th prime factor of an integer n (where k>=1), counted with multiplicity. This is defined as the k-th element (0-indexed k-1) of `Nat.primeFactorsList n.natAbs`. Returns 1 if n has fewer than k prime factors or if n is 0, 1, or -1, following the informal convention.

@file APNOutputs/OEIS/oeis_103311_conjecture_0.lean
@decl a
-- L4DEL: title: A103311: A transform of the Fibonacci numbers
-- L4DEL: intuition: A103311: A transform of the Fibonacci numbers. The sequence $a(n)$ satisfies the linear recurrence relation: $$a(n) = 3a(n-1) - 4a(n-2) + 2a(n-3) - a(n-4)$$ with initial terms $a(0)=0, a(1)=1, a(2)=1, a(3)=0$. The sequence takes values in $\mathbb{Z}$.

